 = 2 (smallest legal radix) and radix = 36 (largest) → boundary of `radix < 2 \\|\\| radix > 36` (jsnumber.c:39) | [ ] |\n| 183 | `Np_toString` | receiver 0.0625, radix = 2 → `point <= 0` → `\"0.\"` + padding zeros + digits → `\"0.0001\"` (jsnumber.c:89-95) | [ ] |\n| 184 | `Np_toString` | receiver 255.5, radix = 16 → `point > 0` with `ndigits > point` → `'.'` inserted mid-digits → `\"ff.8\"` (jsnumber.c:96-101) | [ ] |\n| 185 | `Np_toString` | receiver 1e21, radix = 36 → `point > ndigits` after trailing-zero trimming → the `while (point-- > 0)` zero-fill tail (jsnumber.c:68-71, 102-103) | [ ] |\n| 186 | `Np_toString` | receiver 35, radix = 36 → single digit `'z'` from the `digits[]` table (jsnumber.c:44, 75-78) | [ ] |\n| 187 | `Np_toString` | receiver 1e300, radix = 2 → the `while (number * pow(radix, exp) > limit)` scaling loop (limit = 2^52) runs many negative steps (jsnumber.c:60-65) | [ ] |\n| 188 | `Np_toString` | receiver 5e-324, radix = 2 → the `while (number * pow(radix, exp+1) < limit)` loop runs many positive steps (jsnumber.c:63-64) | [ ] |\n| 189 | `Np_toString` | receiver 4096, radix = 2 → `u % radix == 0` repeatedly → the trailing-zero trim loop drives `exp` down (jsnumber.c:68-71) | [ ] |\n| 190 | `numtostr` (via `Np_toFixed`) | fmt = `\"%.*f\"`, width = 2, x = 1.005 → no `'e'` in the sprintf output → `strchr` returns NULL, buffer used as-is (jsnumber.c:119-125) | [ ] |\n| 191 | `numtostr` (via `Np_toExponential`) | fmt = `\"%.*e\"`, width = 2, x = 1e-7 → output contains `'e'` → exponent re-normalised with `sprintf(e, \"e%+d\", atoi(e+1))`, dropping platform zero-padding (`e-07` → `e-7`) (jsnumber.c:120-124) | [ ] |\n| 192 | `numtostr` (via `Np_toPrecision`) | fmt = `\"%.*g\"`, width = 21, x = 1e20 → `%g` may or may not produce an exponent depending on magnitude; test both 1e20 (no `'e'`) and 1e21 (`'e'` present) (jsnumber.c:119-124, 173) | [ ] |\n| 193 | `numtostr` | fmt = `\"%.20f\"`, x = 1e20 → the widest output the `char buf[50]` is sized for (21 integer digits + `'.'` + 20 fraction digits = 42) (jsnumber.c:117-119) | [ ] |\n| 194 | `Np_toFixed` | receiver 1.5, width = 0 (lower bound, `width < 0` false) and width = 20 (upper bound, `width > 20` false) (jsnumber.c:135-136, 141) | [ ] |\n| 195 | `Np_toFixed` | receiver 1e21 exactly (`x >= 1e21`), width = 2 → bypasses `%f` and uses `jsV_numbertostring` (jsnumber.c:138-139) | [ ] |\n| 196 | `Np_toFixed` | receiver -1e21 exactly (`x <= -1e21`) and receiver 9.999999999999999e20 (just under the bound, so `%f` is used) (jsnumber.c:138) | [ ] |\n| 197 | `Np_toFixed` | receiver NaN and receiver Infinity, width = 2 → `isnan`/`isinf` → `jsV_numbertostring` (jsnumber.c:138) | [ ] |\n| 198 | `Np_toExponential` | receiver 0, width = 0 (lower bound) and width = 20 (upper bound); receiver NaN/Infinity → `jsV_numbertostring` (jsnumber.c:151-157) | [ ] |\n| 199 | `Np_toPrecision` | receiver 123.456, width = 1 (lower bound, `width < 1` false) and width = 21 (upper bound, `width > 21` false — note this differs from toFixed/toExponential's 20) (jsnumber.c:167-168, 173) | [ ] |\n| 200 | `Np_toPrecision` | receiver NaN / Infinity, width = 5 → `jsV_numbertostring` bypass (jsnumber.c:170-171) | [ ] |\n\n## jslex.c — helper predicates\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 201 | `jsY_iswhite` | c ∈ {0x9, 0xB, 0xC, 0x20, 0xA0, 0xFEFF} → 1 (each of the six literals is a separate `==` in the disjunction) (jslex.c:109-112) | [ ] |\n| 202 | `jsY_iswhite` | c ∈ {0xA, 0xD, 0x0, 0x41, 0xA1, 0x2028, 0xFEFE} → 0 (near-miss neighbours of every accepted constant) (jslex.c:111) | [ ] |\n| 203 | `jsY_isnewline` | c ∈ {0xA, 0xD, 0x2028, 0x2029} → 1 (jslex.c:114-117) | [ ] |\n| 204 | `jsY_isnewline` | c ∈ {0x9, 0xB, 0xC, 0x2027, 0x202A} → 0 (jslex.c:116) | [ ] |\n| 205 | `jsY_ishex` | c ∈ {`'0'`, `'9'`, `'a'`, `'f'`, `'A'`, `'F'`} → 1; c ∈ {`'/'`, `':'`, `'g'`, `'G'`, `'`'`, `'@'`} → 0 (both the `isdigit` and the `ishex` sub-ranges plus all four fence-posts) (jslex.c:123, 126, 144-147) | [ ] |\n| 206 | `jsY_tohex` | c = `'0'`→0, `'9'`→9 (first branch); `'a'`→10, `'f'`→15 (second); `'A'`→10, `'F'`→15 (third) (jslex.c:151-153) | [ ] |\n| 207 | `jsY_tohex` | c = `'g'` / `'G'` / `'Z'` / 0 → falls through all three tests and returns 0 (indistinguishable from `'0'`; callers must pre-check with `jsY_ishex`) (jslex.c:154) | [ ] |\n| 208 | `jsY_findword` | s = `\"if\"`, list = keywords, num = 29 → hit; check hits on the first (`\"break\"`), last (`\"with\"`), and mid (`\"in\"`) entries to exercise both `l = m+1` and `r = m-1` (jslex.c:81-95) | [ ] |\n| 209 | `jsY_findword` | s = `\"aaa\"` (sorts before all) and s = `\"zzz\"` (sorts after all) → -1, exiting via `l > r` from either end (jslex.c:85-95) | [ ] |\n| 210 | `jsY_findword` | s = `\"brea\"` (proper prefix of a keyword, `strcmp < 0`) and s = `\"breakx\"` (`strcmp > 0`) → -1 (jslex.c:87-91) | [ ] |\n| 211 | `jsY_findword` | num = 0 → `r = -1`, loop never entered → -1 (jslex.c:83-95) | [ ] |\n| 212 | `jsY_tokenstring` | token = 0 → `\"(end-of-file)\"`; token = 65 (`'A'`) → `\"'A'\"`; token = 0x7F → `\"'\\\\x7F'\"` (jslex.c:24-40, 66-71) | [ ] |\n| 213 | `jsY_tokenstring` | token = 0x80..0xFF → the entry is a literal `0` in the table, so the `if (tokenstring[token])` test fails → `\"<unknown>\"` (jslex.c:42-49, 69) | [ ] |\n| 214 | `jsY_tokenstring` | token = TK_IDENTIFIER / TK_NUMBER / TK_STRING / TK_REGEXP (0x100..0x103) → the named entries after the hole (jslex.c:51) | [ ] |\n| 215 | `jsY_tokenstring` | token = TK_WITH (last table entry) vs token = TK_WITH+1 (`>= nelem(tokenstring)`) → `\"'with'\"` vs `\"<unknown>\"` (jslex.c:63, 68) | [ ] |\n| 216 | `jsY_tokenstring` | token = -1 (`token >= 0` false) → `\"<unknown>\"` (jslex.c:68) | [ ] |\n\n## jslex.c — jsY_initlex / jsY_lex / jsY_lexjson\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 217 | `jsY_initlex` | source = `\"\"` → the priming `jsY_next` sees `*J->source == 0` → `lexchar = EOF`; also sets `line = 1`, `lasttoken = 0` (jslex.c:157-161, 733-740) | [ ] |\n| 218 | `jsY_initlex` / `jsY_next` | source = `\"\\r\\nx\"` → CR followed by LF is consumed as one unit, `line` incremented exactly once, `lexchar` normalised to `'\\n'` (jslex.c:166-171) | [ ] |\n| 219 | `jsY_initlex` / `jsY_next` | source = `\"\\rx\"` (lone CR, `*J->source != '\\n'`) and source = `\"\\u2028x\"` → both normalise to `'\\n'` and bump `line` (jslex.c:166-171) | [ ] |\n| 220 | `jsY_initlex` / `jsY_next` | source = `\"\\xE2\\x82\\xACx\"` (multi-byte lead) → `chartorune` advances `J->source` by 3 (jslex.c:164) | [ ] |\n| 221 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"\\t\\v\\f  1\"` → the `jsY_iswhite` skip loop then a number token; `J->newline` stays 0 (jslex.c:553, 558-559) | [ ] |\n| 222 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"return\\n1\"` → newline with `lasttoken` in `isnlthcontext` (TK_BREAK/TK_CONTINUE/TK_RETURN/TK_THROW) → synthetic `';'` returned (jslex.c:538-548, 561-565) | [ ] |\n| 223 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"a\\n1\"` → newline with a non-NLTH `lasttoken` → `newline = 1` and `continue` (no token emitted) (jslex.c:561-566) | [ ] |\n| 224 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"// c\\n1\"` → `'/'` then `'/'` → `lexlinecomment` runs to `'\\n'` or EOF (jslex.c:568-571, 229-233) | [ ] |\n| 225 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"/* a ** b */1\"` → `lexcomment`, including the inner `while (J->lexchar == '*')` run of consecutive stars (jslex.c:235-249, 572-574) | [ ] |\n| 226 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"/re/gim\"` with `lasttoken = 0` (a regexp context per `isregexpcontext`) → `lexregexp`, flags g\\|i\\|m (jslex.c:458-475, 477-534, 576-577) | [ ] |\n| 227 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"/=2\"` with `lasttoken = TK_IDENTIFIER` (NOT a regexp context) → `TK_DIV_ASS` (jslex.c:461-471, 578-579) | [ ] |\n| 228 | `jsY_lex` | source = `\"/2\"` with `lasttoken = ')'` → plain `'/'` division token (jslex.c:462, 580-582) | [ ] |\n| 229 | `jsY_lex` → `lexnumber` | source = `\"0\"`, `\"5\"`, `\"0.5\"`, `\"5.25\"` → the three mantissa shapes at jslex.c:345-368 (`accept('0')` / `accept('.')` / default digit run), reaching `js_strtod(s, NULL)` (jslex.c:383, 585-586) | [ ] |\n| 230 | `jsY_lex` → `lexnumber` | source = `\"0x1F\"` / `\"0X1f\"` → `lexhex` accumulates via `jsY_ishex`/`jsY_tohex` and returns early without touching `js_strtod` (jslex.c:251-261, 346-349) | [ ] |\n| 231 | `jsY_lex` → `lexnumber` | source = `\".5\"` (digit follows the dot → number) vs source = `\".x\"` (no digit → the `'.'` punctuation token) (jslex.c:356-358, 606-607) | [ ] |\n| 232 | `jsY_lex` → `lexnumber` | source = `\"1e5\"` / `\"1E5\"` / `\"1e+5\"` / `\"1e-5\"` → exponent marker plus the optional sign (jslex.c:370-378) | [ ] |\n| 233 | `jsY_lex` → `lexstring` | source = `\"'a'\"` (single quote) and `\"\\\"a\\\"\"` (double quote) → `q` is captured from `lexchar`, so the closing delimiter must match (jslex.c:429-455, 602-604) | [ ] |\n| 234 | `jsY_lex` → `lexescape` | source = `\"'\\\\n\\\\t\\\\b\\\\f\\\\r\\\\v\\\\0\\\\\\\\\\\\'\\\\\\\"'\"` → every named single-character escape plus `\\0` and the `default:` passthrough (jslex.c:414-425) | [ ] |\n| 235 | `jsY_lex` → `lexescape` | source = `\"'\\\\u0041\\\\x41'\"` → the 4-hex-digit and 2-hex-digit escape branches (jslex.c:400-413) | [ ] |\n| 236 | `jsY_lex` → `lexescape` | source = `\"'a\\\\\\n b'\"` (backslash-newline line continuation) → `jsY_accept(J, '\\n')` returns 0 immediately with nothing pushed (jslex.c:395-396) | [ ] |\n| 237 | `jsY_lex` → `lexregexp` | source = `\"/[/]/\"` → `'['` sets `inclass`, so the enclosed `'/'` does not terminate the body; `']'` clears it (jslex.c:488, 502-505) | [ ] |\n| 238 | `jsY_lex` → `lexregexp` | source = `\"/a\\\\/b/\"` (escaped slash → literal `'/'`) vs `\"/a\\\\db/\"` (backslash kept plus the next char) (jslex.c:491-500) | [ ] |\n| 239 | `jsY_lex` → `lexregexp` | source = `\"/a/\"` (no flags → `J->