{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_wrzlo2uikdly4oqq4butx3l3xiwd4hs53zdqqdfebwzcnr3qcbja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01XhgVZx2GxMdGSrsgw72K2C","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jqRrno/translation/CONFIGS_part2.md","content":"# CONFIGS — Part 2: regexp engine\n\nScope: `c_src/src/regexp.c`, `c_src/src/regexp.h`, `c_src/src/jsregexp.c`.\n\nConstants derived from source: `REPINF = 255` (regexp.c:16), `REG_MAXPROG = 32<<10`\n(regexp.c:18), `REG_MAXREC = 4096` (regexp.c:21), `REG_MAXSPAN = 64` (regexp.c:24),\n`REG_MAXCLASS = 128` (regexp.c:27), `REG_MAXSUB = 16` (regexp.h:35),\n`REG_ICASE = 1`, `REG_NEWLINE = 2`, `REG_NOTBOL = 4` (regexp.h:22-29),\n`JS_REGEXP_G = 1`, `JS_REGEXP_I = 2`, `JS_REGEXP_M = 4` (include/mujs.h:82-84).\n\nEngine semantics established by reading the source (used to pick the expected results below):\n\n* `js_regexec` returns `0` = match, `1` = no match, `-1` = recursion limit (regexp.c:1080-1081,\n  1222, 1075-1076).\n* The compiler prepends `split / anynl / jump` (regexp.c:967-972) with `split->x = split+3`\n  tried *before* `split->y`, so the search is **leftmost** and advances one *rune* at a time,\n  crossing newlines (`I_ANYNL`, regexp.c:1115-1119).\n* `I_SPLIT` recurses into `pc->x` first and returns on the first success (regexp.c:1085-1095),\n  so the engine is **leftmost-first (backtracking/Perl)**, *not* POSIX leftmost-longest.\n* `REG_ICASE` acts at compile time on `I_CHAR` (regexp.c:792) and at run time on\n  `I_CHAR`/`I_CCLASS`/`I_NCCLASS`/`I_REF` (regexp.c:1130, 1139, 1151, 1162).\n* `REG_NEWLINE` affects **only** `I_BOL`/`I_EOL` (regexp.c:1179, 1191). There is no dotall:\n  `I_ANY` always rejects newlines (regexp.c:1123).\n* `REG_NOTBOL` affects **only** `I_BOL` (regexp.c:1175).\n* `eflags` are OR-ed into `prog->flags` at exec time (regexp.c:1239), so `REG_ICASE`/`REG_NEWLINE`\n  are also accepted (with partial effect) as exec flags.\n* Group 0 is the whole match, so a pattern may hold at most **15** capturing groups\n  (`g->nsub` starts at 1, regexp.c:931; `die` at regexp.c:551-552).\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n\n## A. Compile flags (`cflags`) on `js_regcomp` / `js_regcompx`\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=0 (no flags), pattern `abc`, eflags=0; subject `\"xabcx\"` → match, sub[0] = offsets 1..4; subject `\"abd\"` → no match. Baseline default-flag path, `g->prog->flags = cflags` (regexp.c:935), `js_regcomp` wrapper regexp.c:1007 | [ ] |\n| 2 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), pattern `abc`, eflags=0; subject `\"ABC\"` → match (compile-time `canon` regexp.c:792 + run-time `canon` regexp.c:1130-1131); subject `\"abd\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 3 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), pattern `[a-z]+`, eflags=0; subject `\"ABC\"` → match (`incclasscanon` regexp.c:1139-1141, 1041-1049); subject `\"123\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 4 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), pattern `[^a]`, eflags=0; subject `\"A\"` → **no** match (negated class folds too, regexp.c:1151-1153); subject `\"b\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 5 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), pattern `(a)\\1`, eflags=0; subject `\"aA\"` → match (`strncmpcanon` regexp.c:1162-1164, 1051-1065); with cflags=0 the same subject → no match (plain `strncmp` regexp.c:1166) | [ ] |\n| 6 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), pattern `É` (É), eflags=0; subject `\"é\"` (UTF-8 C3 A9) → match via `canon`/`toupperrune` (regexp.c:73-79); subject `\"e\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 7 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), pattern `ſ` (ſ), eflags=0; subject `\"ſ\"` → match; subject `\"s\"`/`\"S\"` → no match — `canon` keeps runes ≥128 whose uppercase is <128 unfolded (regexp.c:76-77) | [ ] |\n| 8 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), pattern `^b`, eflags=0; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at offset 2 (regexp.c:1179-1183); same pattern with cflags=0 → no match | [ ] |\n| 9 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), pattern `a$`, eflags=0; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at offset 0 (regexp.c:1191-1195); with cflags=0 → no match | [ ] |\n| 10 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), pattern `a.b`, eflags=0; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → **no** match — `REG_NEWLINE` does not make `.` match newlines (regexp.c:1120-1126); subject `\"axb\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 11 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), pattern `^x` , eflags=0, subject `\"a\u2028x\"` → match — `isnewline` accepts U+2028/U+2029 (regexp.c:1019-1022, 1180); subject `\"a x\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 12 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE\\|REG_NEWLINE(3), pattern `^abc$`, eflags=0; subject `\"x\\nABC\\ny\"` → match; subject `\"x\\nABD\\ny\"` → no match. Both flag bits live in `prog->flags` (regexp.c:935) and are consulted independently | [ ] |\n| 13 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2) on a pattern containing no `I_BOL`/`I_EOL`: pattern `abc`, eflags=0, subject `\"abc\"` → match; behaviour bit-for-bit identical to cflags=0 (no opcode reads the flag) | [ ] |\n\n## B. JS-level flags and their mapping to `cflags` (`jsregexp.c`)\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 14 | js_newregexp | flags=0 (no g/i/m), pattern `abc` → `opts = 0` (jsregexp.c:32-34), `js_regcompx(J->alloc, J->actx, \"abc\", 0, &error)` (jsregexp.c:36); `/abc/.exec(\"abc\")` → match | [ ] |\n| 15 | js_newregexp + js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=JS_REGEXP_G(1), pattern `a` → `opts = 0`; `re->last` bookkeeping enabled (jsregexp.c:62-73, 88-89). Subject `\"aa\"`: first exec → index 0, `last=1`; second exec → index 1 | [ ] |\n| 16 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_I(2), pattern `abc` → `opts = REG_ICASE` (jsregexp.c:33); subject `\"ABC\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 17 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_M(4), pattern `^b` → `opts = REG_NEWLINE` (jsregexp.c:34); subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 18 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_I(3), pattern `a` → `opts = REG_ICASE`; subject `\"xA\"` → match at index 1, `last=2` | [ ] |\n| 19 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_M(5), pattern `^b` → `opts = REG_NEWLINE`; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at index 2 | [ ] |\n| 20 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_I\\|JS_REGEXP_M(6), pattern `^b$` → `opts = REG_ICASE\\|REG_NEWLINE` (3); subject `\"a\\nB\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 21 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_I\\|JS_REGEXP_M(7), pattern `^b$` → `opts = 3` plus lastIndex tracking; subject `\"a\\nB\\nb\"` → two successive matches | [ ] |\n| 22 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G, `re->last == 0`, pattern `a`, subject `\"ab\"` → `opts = 0`, `haystack == text` (jsregexp.c:68 not taken) | [ ] |\n| 23 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G (no M), `re->last = 2`, pattern `^a`, subject `\"xxab\"` → `haystack = text+2`, `opts \\|= REG_NOTBOL` (jsregexp.c:69-72) → **no** match even though `haystack[0]=='a'` | [ ] |\n| 24 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G\\|M, `re->last = 2`, pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → `haystack[-1] == '\\n'` so `REG_NOTBOL` is **not** set (jsregexp.c:70) → match at index 2 | [ ] |\n| 25 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G\\|M, `re->last = 2`, pattern `^c`, subject `\"abc\"` → `haystack[-1] == 'b'` so `REG_NOTBOL` **is** set (jsregexp.c:70-71) → no match | [ ] |\n| 26 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G, `re->last = 5 > strlen(\"abc\")` → `last` reset to 0 and `null` pushed without calling `js_regexec` (jsregexp.c:63-67) | [ ] |\n| 27 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G, `re->last == strlen(text)` (== 3, boundary, `>` is false), pattern `x*`, subject `\"abc\"` → `haystack = text+3` (empty), `REG_NOTBOL` set, empty match at end | [ ] |\n| 28 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `(b)(c)`, subject `\"abc\"` → result array holds `m.nsub` (= 3) elements via the `for (i = 0; i < m.nsub; ++i)` loop (jsregexp.c:84-87); `nsub` comes from `prog->nsub` (regexp.c:953, 1235) | [ ] |\n| 29 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `b`, non-ASCII subject `\"ééb\"` (UTF-8) → `index` property is a *rune* index (2) via `js_utfptrtoidx` (jsregexp.c:82), not the byte offset (4) | [ ] |\n| 30 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `(a)\\|(b)` (alternation with an unmatched group), subject `\"b\"` → unset `sub[1]` has `sp==ep==NULL` (regexp.c:1236-1237) so `js_pushlstring(NULL, 0)` yields `\"\"` (jsregexp.c:85) | [ ] |\n| 31 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec (via Rp_test) | flags=G, pattern `a`, subject `\"xaxa\"` — `Rp_test` accumulates `re->last = re->last + (m.sub[0].ep - text)` on the *advanced* pointer (jsregexp.c:128-129) vs `exec`'s absolute `m.sub[0].ep - text` (jsregexp.c:89); exercise both to confirm identical lastIndex progression | [ ] |\n| 32 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G, zero-width pattern `a*`, subject `\"b\"` → match of length 0, `re->last = 0` (no progress) — repeat-until-null drivers must handle the non-advancing case (jsregexp.c:88-89) | [ ] |\n| 33 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp()` — pattern argument undefined → pattern replaced by `\"(?:)\"`, flags=0 (jsregexp.c:154-156); compiles to the bare prologue and matches everything | [ ] |\n| 34 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp(\"\")` — zero-length pattern → replaced by `\"(?:)\"` (jsregexp.c:162-163) | [ ] |\n| 35 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp(\"a/b\")` — `is_clone == 0` so `source` is run through `escaperegexp` → stored as `a\\/b` (jsregexp.c:4-21, 41) while the *compiled* pattern is the unescaped `a/b`; subject `\"a/b\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 36 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp(/a\\/b/g)` — clone path: `is