{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_km2b3nlqv2tcpeiminxp27hfdzzniujcb35atx2dnoaysj32rija","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01D1P7evbvHRt6Ropc7FGFFH","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jqRrno/translation/CONFIGS_part3.md","content":"# CONFIGS — Part 3: state, runtime, objects, builtins, compiler\n\nConfiguration-surface table for the MuJS state / runtime / object / builtin surface.\nEvery row is an axis the C actually branches on, derived from the sources listed below.\nSources: `include/mujs.h`, `src/jsstate.c`, `src/jsrun.c`, `src/jsvalue.c`, `src/jsproperty.c`,\n`src/jsobject.c`, `src/jsgc.c`, `src/jsintern.c`, `src/jsrepr.c`, `src/jsbuiltin.c`,\n`src/jsarray.c`, `src/jsboolean.c`, `src/jsdate.c`, `src/jserror.c`, `src/jsfunction.c`,\n`src/jsmath.c`, `src/jsnumber.c`, `src/jsstring.c`, `src/jsregexp.c`, `src/json.c`,\n`src/jsparse.c`, `src/jscompile.c`.\n\nCompile-time constants that bound several rows (all from `jsi.h`): `JS_STACKSIZE 4096`\n(jsi.h:81), `JS_ENVLIMIT 1024` (jsi.h:84), `JS_TRYLIMIT 64` (jsi.h:87),\n`JS_ARRAYLIMIT 1<<26` (jsi.h:91), `JS_GCFACTOR 5.0` (jsi.h:103), `JS_ASTLIMIT 400`\n(jsi.h:107), `JS_STRLIMIT 1<<28` (jsi.h:111).\n\n## A. State construction, allocator, limits, reporting, context\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `js_newstate` | `js_newstate(NULL, NULL, 0)` — default allocator (`js_defaultalloc` = realloc/free, jsstate.c:15-22), `actx = NULL`, non-strict: `J->strict == J->default_strict == 0` (jsstate.c:194-205). | [ ] |\n| 2 | `js_newstate` | `js_newstate(NULL, NULL, JS_STRICT)` i.e. flags==1 — sets `J->strict = J->default_strict = 1` (jsstate.c:204-205); all of section B then takes the strict branch by default. | [ ] |\n| 3 | `js_newstate`, `js_malloc`, `js_free` | custom allocator: `js_newstate(myalloc, &myctx, 0)` with `myalloc(memctx,ptr,size)` honoring `size==0` as free; `J->actx = &myctx`, every `js_malloc/js_realloc/js_free` routes through it (jsrun.c:52-92) and `js_regcompx(J->alloc, J->actx, ...)` too (jsbuiltin.c:205). | [ ] |\n| 4 | `js_newstate` | allocator that returns NULL for the first `sizeof(js_State)` request → `js_newstate` returns NULL without touching anything (jsstate.c:197-199). | [ ] |\n| 5 | `js_newstate` | allocator that succeeds for `js_State` but returns NULL for the `JS_STACKSIZE*16` stack allocation → J is freed via `alloc(actx,J,0)` and NULL returned (jsstate.c:214-218). | [ ] |\n| 6 | `js_newstate`, `js_freestate` | allocator that throws/fails during `jsB_init` so the `js_try(J)` at jsstate.c:224 fires → `js_freestate(J)` then NULL. | [ ] |\n| 7 | `js_freestate` | `js_freestate(NULL)` — early return (jsgc.c:267-268). | [ ] |\n| 8 | `js_freestate` | after `js_dostring(J, \"var a=[1,2,3], r=/x/g, u={};\")`: frees envs, funs, objs (incl. regexp prog + CSTRING heap string + flat array), interned strings, lexbuf, stack, J (jsgc.c:260-284). | [ ] |\n| 9 | `js_setlimit` | `js_setlimit(J, 0, 0)` — both counters disabled: `J->runlimit > 0` false (jsrun.c:1602) and `J->memlimit > 0` false (jsrun.c:55, 68) ⇒ unlimited. | [ ] |\n| 10 | `js_setlimit`, `jsR_run` | `js_setlimit(J, 100000, 0)` then `js_dostring(J, \"while(1);\")` — runlimit decrements once per bytecode dispatch, throws `\"script ran too long\"` when it reaches 1 (jsrun.c:38-44, 1602-1606). | [ ] |\n| 11 | `js_setlimit`, `jsR_run` | `js_setlimit(J, 1, 0)` then any script — throws on the very first instruction (jsrun.c:1603). | [ ] |\n| 12 | `js_setlimit`, `js_malloc` | `js_setlimit(J, 0, 4096)` then `js_dostring(J, \"var s='x'; while(1) s+=s;\")` — memlimit is decremented per allocation and never credited back on free (jsrun.c:55-59, 88-92 `TODO`), so `\"out of memory\"` is thrown; single allocation `size >= memlimit` also throws (jsrun.c:56). | [ ] |\n| 13 | `js_setlimit`, `js_realloc` | `js_setlimit(J, 0, 64)` then `js_pushstring(J, <65-byte string>)` — `js_realloc` path with `size >= J->memlimit` (jsrun.c:70-71). | [ ] |\n| 14 | `js_setlimit` | `js_setlimit(J, -1, -1)` — negative values behave as unlimited (both checks are `> 0`, jsrun.c:1602, 55). | [ ] |\n| 15 | `js_gc` | `js_gc(J, 0)` — collect silently; also the implicit call from the interpreter loop when `J->gccounter > J->gcthresh` (jsrun.c:1608-1609). Recomputes `gccounter = remaining`, `gcthresh = remaining*5.0` (jsgc.c:249-250). | [ ] |\n| 16 | `js_gc`, `js_report` | `js_gc(J, 1)` — formats `\"garbage collected (%d%%): ... envs, funs, objs, props, strs\"` into a 256-byte buffer and calls `js_report` (jsgc.c:252-257). Run once on a fresh state (`ntot` non-zero) and once after everything is garbage. | [ ] |\n| 17 | `js_gc`, `js_newuserdata`, `js_newcfunctionx` | GC with unreachable `JS_CUSERDATA` / `JS_CCFUNCTION` objects that have non-NULL `finalize` — finalizer invoked from `jsG_freeobject` (jsgc.c:49-52). | [ ] |\n| 18 | `js_setreport`, `js_report` | `js_setreport(J, NULL)` then trigger a warning/report — `js_report` becomes a no-op (jsstate.c:166-170). | [ ] |\n| 19 | `js_setreport`, `js_report` | `js_setreport(J, mycb)` then `js_dostring(J, \"syntax error here\")` — message routed to `mycb`; contrast with the default `js_defaultreport` writing to stderr with a trailing newline (jsstate.c:24-28). | [ ] |\n| 20 | `js_atpanic`, `js_throw` | default panic: `js_throw(J)` with `J->trytop == 0` → `js_defaultpanic` reports `\"uncaught exception\"` then `abort()` (jsstate.c:30-34, jsrun.c:1479-1481). | [ ] |\n| 21 | `js_atpanic` | `old = js_atpanic(J, mypanic)` — returns the previous handler (`js_defaultpanic` on a fresh state) and installs the new one (jsstate.c:159-164); then `js_atpanic(J, NULL)` so `J->panic` is NULL and `js_throw` goes straight to `abort()` (jsrun.c:1479). | [ ] |\n| 22 | `js_setcontext`, `js_getcontext` | `js_setcontext(J, p); assert(js_getcontext(J) == p);` for `p == NULL` and `p == &blob` (jsstate.c:177-185); `J->uctx` starts NULL from the `memset` at jsstate.c:200. | [ ] |\n| 23 | `js_dostring` | `js_dostring(J, \"1+1\")` (returns 0) vs `js_dostring(J, \"throw new Error('x')\")` (reports via `js_trystring(J,-1,\"Error\")`, returns 1) (jsstate.c:139-157). | [ ] |\n| 24 | `js_dostring`, `js_ploadstring`, `js_ptry` | drive `J->trytop` to `JS_TRYLIMIT` (64) with nested `js_try`, then call `js_dostring`/`js_ploadstring`/`js_trystring`/`js_trynumber`/`js_tryinteger`/`js_tryboolean` — each takes the `js_ptry` overflow branch pushing the literal `\"exception stack overflow\"` (jsstate.c:5-13, 36-109). | [ ] |\n| 25 | `js_ploadstring`, `js_loadstring`, `js_loadeval` | `js_ploadstring(J, \"f.js\", \"func(\")` (syntax error → returns 1) vs valid source (returns 0 leaving a `JS_CSCRIPT` object). `js_loadstring` compiles with `J->default_strict` and scope `J->GE`; `js_loadeval` compiles with the *current* `J->strict` and scope `J->strict ? J->E : NULL` (jsstate.c:111-137). | [ ] |\n| 26 | `js_pcall`, `js_pconstruct` | `js_getglobal(J,\"f\"); js_pushundefined(J); js_pcall(J,0)` for an `f` that returns normally (0) and for one that throws (1, stack trimmed to just the error at `savetop`) (jsrun.c:1400-1426). | [ ] |\n| 27 | `js_savetry`, `js_savetrypc`, `js_endtry`, `js_throw` | `js_savetry` (pc = NULL) vs `js_savetrypc(J, pc)` (pc recorded, used by `OP_TRY`, jsrun.c:2027-2034); both snapshot `E, envtop, tracetop, top, bot, strict` (jsrun.c:1430-1456); `js_endtry` with `trytop == 0` → `\"endtry: exception stack underflow\"` (jsrun.c:1458-1463); `js_throw` restores all six fields (jsrun.c:1465-1478). | [ ] |\n| 28 | `js_savetry` | 64 nested `js_try` then one more → `js_trystackoverflow` (jsrun.c:14-20, 1446). | [ ] |\n| 29 | `js_trap` | `js_dostring(J, \"debugger;\")` → `OP_DEBUGGER` calls `js_trap` which dumps every stack slot (with `>` marking BOT) plus the trace (jsrun.c:1546-1563, 2068-2070). Exercise with each value shape on the stack so every `js_dumpvalue` case is hit (jsrun.c:1486-1526), including `v.u.object == J->G` → `[Global]`. | [ ] |\n| 30 | `jsS_dumpstrings`, `jsS_freestrings`, `js_intern` | fresh state: `jsS_dumpstrings(J)` with `J->strings == NULL` (empty tree, jsintern.c:108-115); then `js_intern(J,\"b\"); js_intern(J,\"a\"); js_intern(J,\"c\"); js_intern(J,\"a\")` (dedupe returns the same pointer, jsintern.c:130-137) and dump again; `jsS_freestrings` on empty vs populated (jsintern.c:124-128). | [ ] |\n| 31 | `js_intern` | `js_intern` with a string longer than `JS_STRLIMIT` → RangeError `\"invalid string length\"` (jsintern.c:46-47). | [ ] |\n| 32 | `js_putc`, `js_puts`, `js_putm` | `js_Buffer *sb = NULL; js_putc(J,&sb,'a')` (first call allocates, `m = 64`), then push >64 bytes so the `sb->n == sb->m` doubling branch fires (jsintern.c:5-18); `js_puts(J,&sb,\"\")` (no-op) vs non-empty; `js_putm(J,&sb,s,s)` (empty range) vs `js_putm(J,&sb,s,s+3)` (jsintern.c:20-30). | [ ] |\n| 33 | `js_malloc`, `js_realloc`, `js_free`, `js_strdup` | `js_strdup(J,\"\")` (1 byte) and `js_strdup(J,\"abc\")`; `js_realloc(J, NULL, 32)` (fresh alloc path) vs growing an existing block; allocator returning NULL → `\"out of memory\"` thrown (jsrun.c:52-92). | [ ] |\n\n## B. Strict-mode-dependent behaviours (one row per distinct branch on `J->strict` / `F->strict`)\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 34 | `js_setvar` (OP_SETVAR) | assignment to an undeclared variable: `js_dostring(J, \"undeclared = 1;\")`. Non-strict: creates a property on `J->G`. Strict: ReferenceError `\"assignment to undeclared variable 'undeclared'\"` (jsrun.c:1132-1134). | [ ] |\n| 35 | `js_setvar` | assignment to a read-only binding: `js_dostring(J, \"NaN = 1;\")` / `\"undefined = 1;\"` (globals defined `JS_READONLY|JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF`, jsbuiltin.c:232-238). Non-strict: silent. Strict: TypeError `\"'NaN' is read-only\"` (jsrun.c:1124-1128). | [ ] |\n| 36 | `js_delvar` (OP_DELVAR/OP_DELLOCAL) | `js_dostring(J, \"var x=1; (function(){ return delete x; })()\")` — env var is `JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF` (`js_initvar`, jsrun.c:1087). Non-strict: returns false. Strict: TypeError `\"'x' is non-configurable\"` (jsrun.c:1143-1147). Note the compiler additionally rejects `delete <identifier>` in strict at compile time (row 47). | [ ] |\n| 37 | `jsR_setproperty` | write through an accessor that has only a getter: `js_dostring(J, \"var o={get a(){return 1}}; o.a = 2;\")`. Non-strict: silent no-op. Strict: TypeError `\"setting property 'a' that only has a getter\"` (jsrun.c:771-773). | [ ] |\n| 38 | `jsR_setproperty` | write to a `JS_READONLY` own/inherited data property: `js_dostring(J, \"var o={}; Object.defineProperty(o,'a',{value:1,writable:false}); o.a=2;\")`. Non-strict: silent. Strict: TypeError `\"'a' is read-only\"` (jsrun.c:774-775, 790-800). | [ ] |\n| 39 | `jsR_setproperty`, `js_setproperty` | write to a *transient* object (primitive receiver): `js_dostring(J, \"'abc'.foo = 1;\")`, or C-side `js_pushstring(J,\"abc\"); js_pushnumber(J,1); js_setproperty(J,-2,\"foo\")` (transient = `!js_isobject`, jsrun.c:1011). Non-strict: silent. Strict: TypeError `\"cannot create property 'foo' on transient object\"` (jsrun.c:780-785). | [ ] |\n| 40 | `jsR_setproperty` | write to a built-in read-only slot: `js_dostring(J, \"'abc'.length = 9;\")` (CSTRING length / index, jsrun.c:736-742) and `js_dostring(J, \"/x/.source='y';\")` (CREGEXP source/global/ignoreCase/multiline, jsrun.c:744-748) — reaches `readonly:` (jsrun.c:798-800). | [ ] |\n| 41 | `jsR_defproperty` | define a value over a `JS_READONLY` property: `js_pushnumber(J,1); js_defproperty(J, idx, \"ro\", 0)` where `ro` already has `JS_READONLY`. Non-strict: value silently dropped. Strict: TypeError `\"'ro' is read-only\"` (jsrun.c:850-855). | [ ] |\n| 42 | `jsR_defproperty`, `js_defaccessor` | install a getter and/or setter over a `JS_DONTCONF` property: `js_newcfunction(...); js_pushnull(J); js_defaccessor(J, idx, \"frozen\", 0)` after `Object.freeze`. Non-strict: silently ignored. Strict: TypeError `\"'frozen' is non-configurable\"` (jsrun.c:856-867). | [ ] |\n| 43 | `jsR_defproperty` | `throw` argument: `js_defproperty`/`js_defaccessor` pass `throw = 1`, `js_defglobal`, `js_initvar`, `OP_INITGETTER`, `OP_INITSETTER` pass `throw = 0` (jsrun.c:995, 1017, 1028, 1087, 1744, 1751). Test `js_newarray(J); js_pushnumber(J,1); js_defproperty(J,-2,\"length\",0)` — array `length` hits `readonly:` which throws when `J->strict || throw` (jsrun.c:820-822, 873-875), so it throws even in non-strict via `js_defproperty` but not via `js_defglobal`. | [ ] |\n| 44 | `jsR_delproperty` | delete a `JS_DONTCONF` property or a built-in slot: `js_dostring(J, \"var o={}; Object.seal(o); delete o.a;\")`, `\"delete [].length\"`, `\"delete 'ab'[0]\"`, `\"delete /x/.lastIndex\"` (jsrun.c:883-909, 911-916). Non-strict: returns false. Strict: TypeError `\"'...' is non-configurable\"` (jsrun.c:919-922). | [ ] |\n| 45 | `jsV_setproperty` | add a property to a non-extensible object: `js_dostring(J, \"var o=Object.preventExtensions({}); o.a=1;\")`. Non-strict: returns NULL, silently ignored. Strict: TypeError `\"object is non-extensible\"` (jsproperty.c:225-230). | [ ] |\n| 46 | `jsR_run` / `OP_THIS` | `this` coercion: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(){ return typeof this; } f();\")`. Non-strict: `this` is not coercible ⇒ replaced by the global object (`js_pushglobal`). Strict: the raw (undefined) value is copied (jsrun.c:1651-1660). | [ ] |\n| 47 | `jsR_callfunction`, `js_newarguments` | `arguments.callee`: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(){ return arguments.callee; } f();\")`. Non-strict: `callee` defined `JS_DONTENUM`. Strict: not defined at all (jsrun.c:1203-1217). `length` is always defined. | [ ] |\n| 48 | `jsV_toprimitive` | object with no callable `toString`/`valueOf`: `js_dostring(J, \"var o = Object.create(null); '' + o;\")`. Non-strict: yields the literal string `\"[object]\"`. Strict: TypeError `\"cannot convert object to primitive\"` (jsvalue.c:117-149). | [ ] |\n| 49 | `jsC_compilescript`, `cfunbody` | `'use strict'` directive prologue: `js_dostring(J, \"'use strict'; undeclared = 1;\")` and the function-level form `\"function f(){ 'use strict'; undeclared=1; } f();\"` — only checked when the *first* statement of the body is a string literal equal to `use strict` (jscompile.c:1382-1385). Also test a non-first `'use strict'` (no effect). | [ ] |\n| 50 | `checkfutureword` | strict future reserved words `implements interface let package private protected public static yield` (jscompile.c:35-38): `js_dostring(J, \"var yield = 1;\")` — non-strict OK, strict SyntaxError `\"'yield' is a strict mode future reserved word\"` (jscompile.c:44-46). Contrast the always-rejected `futurewords` list `class const enum export extends import super` (jscompile.c:31-33, 42-43). | [ ] |\n| 51 | `addlocal` | `arguments` as a declared name: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(){ var arguments; }\")` — strict SyntaxError `\"redefining 'arguments' is not allowed in strict mode\"` (jscompile.c:112-114); non-strict accepted. | [ ] |\n| 52 | `addlocal` | `eval` as a declared name: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(eval){}\")` — strict: `\"redefining 'eval' is not allowed in strict mode\"`; non-strict: EvalError `\"invalid use of 'eval'\"` (jscompile.c:112-119) — *both* reject, with different error classes. | [ ] |\n| 53 | `addlocal` | duplicate formal parameter: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(a,a){}\")` — strict SyntaxError `\"duplicate formal parameter 'a'\"`; non-strict accepted (jscompile.c:121-131). Also `reuse=1` path from `cfunbody` binding a named function expression (jscompile.c:1391-1398). | [ ] |\n| 54 | `emitlocal` | assigning to `arguments` / `eval`: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(){ arguments = 1; }\")` and `\"function f(){ eval = 1; }\")` — strict SyntaxError `\"'arguments' is read-only in strict mode\"` / `\"'eval' is read-only in strict mode\"` (jscompile.c:202-207). Note `eval` as an identifier at all triggers EvalError at jscompile.c:208-209. | [ ] |\n| 55 | `cobject`, `checkdup` | duplicate property in an object literal: `js_dostring(J, \"var o = {a:1, a:2};\")` and the numeric-key variant `\"({1:1, 1.0:2})\"` (keys normalized via `jsV_numbertostring`, jscompile.c:297-318) — strict SyntaxError `\"duplicate property 'a' in object literal\"`; non-strict accepted (jscompile.c:339-340). | [ ] |\n| 56 | `cdelete` | `delete` on an unqualified name: `js_dostring(J, \"var x; delete x;\")` — strict SyntaxError `\"delete on an unqualified name is not allowed in strict mode\"` (jscompile.c:506-508); non-strict compiles to `OP_DELLOCAL`/`OP_DELVAR`. | [ ] |\n| 57 | `ctrycatch`, `ctrycatchfinally` | catch parameter named `arguments`/`eval`: `js_dostring(J, \"try{}catch(eval){}\")` and the `try/catch/finally` form — strict SyntaxError (jscompile.c:957-963 and 989-995). Note `checkfutureword` is called unconditionally in `ctrycatch` but only inside the `if (F->strict)` block in `ctrycatchfinally` — a real behavioural difference between the two shapes. | [ ] |\n| 58 | `cstm` / `STM_WITH` | `js_dostring(J, \"with({a:1}) { a; }\")` — non-strict: compiles to `OP_WITH`/`OP_ENDWITH` and forces `F->lightweight = 0`; strict: SyntaxError `\"'with' statements are not allowed in strict mode\"` (jscompile.c:1263-1273). | [ ] |\n| 59 | `lexnumber` | legacy octal literal `js_dostring(J, \"010\")` — **not** strict-dependent in this build: always SyntaxError `\"number with leading zero\"` (jslex.c:311-312/350-351). Also `\"0x10\"` (accepted) and `\"1abc\"` → `\"number with letter suffix\"`. | [ ] |\n| 60 | `jsR_run`, `js_savetry`, `js_throw` | strict flag is per-function and save/restored: `jsR_run` sets `J->strict = F->strict` on entry and restores `savestrict` at `OP_RETURN` (jsrun.c:1594-1595, 2092-2094); `js_savetry`/`js_savetrypc` snapshot it and `js_throw` restores it (jsrun.c:1439, 1453, 1475). Test a non-strict function calling a strict one that throws, caught in the non-strict caller. | [ ] |\n| 61 | `js_loadeval`, `js_eval` (OP_EVAL) | direct `eval`: `js_dostring(J, \"eval('var q=1;'); q;\")` in a non-strict state (compiled with `J->default_strict`, scope NULL ⇒ runs in the caller's env, jsstate.c:122-124, jsrun.c:1237-1264) vs a `JS_STRICT` state (compiled strict, scope `J->E`). Also `js_eval` with a non-string on top of the stack → returns unchanged (jsrun.c:1390-1393). | [ ] |\n\n## C. Property attribute flags (`JS_READONLY=1`, `JS_DONTENUM=2`, `JS_DONTCONF=4`)\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 62 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = 0` — plain writable/enumerable/configurable property; then overwrite, enumerate with `for-in`, and `delete` it (all succeed). | [ ] |\n| 63 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_READONLY (1)` — subsequent `js_setproperty`/JS assignment is dropped (jsrun.c:789-793); `delete` still works; still enumerable. | [ ] |\n| 64 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_DONTENUM (2)` — hidden from `for-in` (`jsV_getenumproperty`/`itwalk`, jsproperty.c:210-219, 252-264), from `Object.keys` (jsobject.c:352-363) and from `propertyIsEnumerable` (jsobject.c:100-106), but still visible to `Object.getOwnPropertyNames` (jsobject.c:157-166) and `hasOwnProperty`. | [ ] |\n| 65 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_DONTCONF (4)` — `delete` fails (jsrun.c:913-914) and getter/setter installation is refused (jsrun.c:857, 863); value writes still allowed. | [ ] |\n| 66 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_READONLY|JS_DONTENUM (3)`. | [ ] |\n| 67 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_READONLY|JS_DONTCONF (5)` — the \"frozen property\" shape (`O_freeze_walk`, jsobject.c:475-482; `O_isFrozen` requires exactly these two, jsobject.c:501-514). | [ ] |\n| 68 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF (6)` — the shape used by `js_initvar` for function-scope variables (jsrun.c:1087) and by `\"prototype\"` on functions (jsvalue.c:470). | [ ] |\n| 69 | `js_defproperty` | `atts = JS_READONLY|JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF (7)` — the shape used for `function.length` (jsvalue.c:464, 505, 531), `jsB_propn` numeric constants (jsbuiltin.c:21), `NaN`/`Infinity`/`undefined` globals (jsbuiltin.c:232-238), `Error.prototype.stack` accessor (jserror.c:121) and `Fp_bind`'s `__TargetFunction__`/`__BoundThis__`/`__BoundArguments__` (jsfunction.c:200-212). | [ ] |\n| 70 | `js_defproperty` | attributes are **accumulated, never cleared**: `js_defproperty(idx,\"a\",JS_READONLY)` then `js_defproperty(idx,\"a\",0)` — `ref->atts |= atts` (jsrun.c:868) so `a` stays read-only. Verify with `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor`. | [ ] |\n| 71 | `js_defglobal`, `js_delglobal`, `js_getglobal`, `js_setglobal` | `js_pushnumber(J,1); js_defglobal(J,\"g\",JS_DONTENUM)` (throw=0 path, jsrun.c:993-997) then `js_getglobal`, `js_setglobal`, `js_delglobal`; repeat with `atts = JS_READONLY|JS_DONTCONF` and observe `js_setglobal`/`js_delglobal` in strict vs non-strict. | [ ] |\n| 72 | `js_defaccessor` | `js_newcfunction(J,getter,\"g\",0); js_newcfunction(J,setter,\"s\",1); js_defaccessor(J, idx, \"p\", 0)` — both getter and setter installed; then the getter-only variant (`js_pushnull` for the setter, as in jserror.c:119-121) and the setter-only variant (`js_pushundefined` for the getter). `jsR_tofunction` accepts undefined/null (→NULL) and functions, else TypeError `\"not a function\"` (jsrun.c:385-394, 1026-1030). | [ ] |\n| 73 | `js_defaccessor`, `jsR_hasproperty`, `jsR_setproperty` | accessor invocation shapes: read through a getter (`js_call(J,0)` with the object as `this`, jsrun.c:632-636), write through a setter (`js_call(J,1)`, jsrun.c:763-769), and read/write an accessor found on the **prototype** rather than the object itself (`jsV_getpropertyx` `own` flag, jsproperty.c:186-197, jsrun.c:761, 780). | [ ] |\n| 74 | `js_defaccessor`, `js_setvar`, `js_hasvar` | accessor on an *environment* record (via `with`): `js_dostring(J, \"var o={get a(){return 1}, set a(v){}}; with(o){ a; a=2; }\")` — exercises the getter/setter branches in `js_hasvar` (jsrun.c:1096-1101) and `js_setvar` (jsrun.c:1116-1122). | [ ] |\n| 75 | `O_getOwnPropertyDescriptor`, `ToPropertyDescriptor` | `Object.defineProperty(o,'a',{value:1,writable:true,enumerable:true,configurable:true})`, then each of the 8 boolean combinations, then `{get:f}`, `{set:f}`, `{get:f,set:g}`, and the error shape `{value:1,get:f}` → TypeError `\"value/writable and get/set attributes are exclusive\"` (jsobject.c:222-273); read back with `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor` (jsobject.c:120-155, including the `undefined` result for a missing property). | [ ] |\n\n## D. Value / data shapes\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 76 | `js_pushundefined`, `js_type`, `js_typeof`, `jsV_toboolean/tonumber/tostring/toobject` | `undefined`: `js_type` → `JS_ISUNDEFINED`, `js_typeof` → `\"undefined\"`, `jsV_toboolean` → 0, `jsV_tonumber` → NaN, `jsV_tostring` → `\"undefined\"`, `jsV_toobject` → TypeError `\"cannot convert undefined to object\"` (jsrun.c:115-120, 286, 305; jsvalue.c:157, 252, 337, 401). | [ ] |\n| 77 | `js_pushnull`, `js_isnull`, `js_iscoercible` | `null`: `js_typeof` → `\"object\"` but `js_type` → `JS_ISNULL`; `js_iscoercible` false; `jsV_tonumber` → 0; `jsV_toobject` → TypeError (jsrun.c:122-127, 242, 287; jsvalue.c:253, 402). | [ ] |\n| 78 | `js_pushboolean`, `js_newboolean` | `js_pushboolean(J, 0)`, `js_pushboolean(J, 1)`, and a non-0/1 truthy int e.g. `js_pushboolean(J, 42)` (normalized via `!!v`, jsrun.c:133); plus the wrapper `js_newboolean(J,1)` (`JS_CBOOLEAN` object, `js_isbooleanobject` true, `jsV_toboolean` → 1 because it is an object). | [ ] |\n| 79 | `js_pushnumber`, `jsV_numbertostring`, `jsV_numbertointeger` | numbers: `0`, `-0.0` (`jsV_numbertostring` returns `\"0\"` at jsvalue.c:275 but `js_repr`/`reprnum` prints `-0`, jsrepr.c:9-12), `NaN`, `+Infinity`, `-Infinity`, `42`, `-42`, `0.5`, `1e21` (point > 21 ⇒ exponent form, jsvalue.c:294-303), `1e-7` (point < -5), `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, `2^31 = 2147483648`, `2^32 = 4294967296`, `2^53 = 9007199254740992`. | [ ] |\n| 80 | `jsV_numbertointeger`, `js_tointeger` | `jsV_numbertointeger` clamping: `0`, `-0`, `NaN` → 0; `0.9` → 0; `-0.9` → -0→0; `1e300` → `INT_MAX`; `-1e300` → `INT_MIN` (jsvalue.c:41-49). | [ ] |\n| 81 | `jsV_numbertoint32`, `js_toint32`, `js_touint32`, `js_toint16`, `js_touint16` | modular conversions: `0`, `NaN`, `Infinity` → 0; `2^31` → `-2147483648`; `2^32` → 0; `2^32+1` → 1; `-1` → `0xFFFFFFFF` for uint32; `65536` and `-1` for the 16-bit variants (jsvalue.c:51-80; jsrun.c:333-351). | [ ] |\n| 82 | `jsV_stringtonumber`, `js_stringtofloat`, `js_strtol` | string→number: `\"\"` → 0, `\"  \\n 12  \"` → 12 (whitespace trimmed both sides), `\"0x1f\"` → 31, `\"0x\"` → NaN (needs `s[2] != 0`), `\"Infinity\"`/`\"+Infinity\"`/`\"-Infinity\"`, `\"1e3\"`, `\".5\"`, `\"1.\"`, `\"12abc\"` → NaN (trailing junk), `\"-7\"` (sign handled outside `js_strtol`) (jsvalue.c:190-244, 7-39). | [ ] |\n| 83 | `js_pushstring`, `js_pushlstring`, `js_pushliteral`, `jsV_newmemstring` | string storage shapes: `js_pushstring(J,\"\")` and `js_pushstring(J,\"abcdefghijklmno\")` (15 bytes = `soffsetof(js_Value,t.type)`, stays `JS_TSHRSTR`, jsrun.c:151-155); 16 bytes → `JS_TMEMSTR` via `jsV_newmemstring` (jsrun.c:157-158, 94-104); `js_pushliteral(J, \"static\")` → `JS_TLITSTR` (jsrun.c:180-186, never GC-scanned). All three type tags must behave identically for `js_isstring`, `js_typeof`, `js_equal`, `js_strictequal` (jsrun.c:239; jsvalue.c:4-5, 652, 698). | [ ] |\n| 84 | `js_pushlstring` | embedded NUL: `js_pushlstring(J, \"a\\0b\", 3)` — 3 bytes copied, terminator appended; observe that everything downstream uses C string semantics (`strlen`, `strcmp`) so the value truncates at the NUL. Also `js_pushlstring(J, buf, 0)` (empty) and `n == 15` vs `n == 16` (shrstr/memstr boundary) (jsrun.c:163-178). | [ ] |\n| 85 | `js_pushstring`, `js_pushlstring` | length limit: `n > JS_STRLIMIT (1<<28)` → RangeError `\"invalid string length\"` (jsrun.c:148-149, 165-166). | [ ] |\n| 86 | `js_newstring`, `jsV_newstring`, `js_utflen` | `JS_CSTRING` object shapes: `js_newstring(J,\"\")`, `js_newstring(J,\"short\")` (≤15 bytes ⇒ inline `u.s.shrstr`, jsvalue.c:385-387), `js_newstring(J,\"<16+ bytes>\")` (heap `js_strdup`, jsvalue.c:389), a multi-byte UTF-8 string (`u.s.length` from `js_utflen`, counting astral chars as 2, jsstring.c:49-71), and one containing an astral plane character (surrogate handling in `js_runeat`, jsstring.c:20-47). | [ ] |\n| 87 | `js_newobject`, `js_newobjectx`, `jsV_newobject` | object shapes: `js_newobject(J)` (prototype = `Object_prototype`, jsvalue.c:424-427); `js_pushobject(J,proto); js_newobjectx(J)` (explicit prototype) vs `js_pushundefined(J); js_newobjectx(J)` (prototype NULL, jsvalue.c:415-422); `jsV_newobject(J, JS_COBJECT, NULL)` used for env records and `catch` scopes (jsrun.c:1199, 2042). | [ ] |\n| 88 | `jsV_setproperty`, `jsV_getownproperty`, `insert`, `skew`, `split` | property-tree (AA-tree) shapes: empty object (`obj->properties == &sentinel`, jsproperty.c:22-27, 175); 1 property; 3 properties inserted in ascending order (forces `skew`+`split` rebalancing, jsproperty.c:60-98); ~64 properties with names `\"p0\"..\"p63\"` inserted ascending, descending, and in random order; then delete interior/root/leaf nodes to drive all three `unlinkproperty` cases and the rebalance block (jsproperty.c:106-163). | [ ] |\n| 89 | `js_newarray`, `jsR_setarrayindex`, `jsR_unflattenarray` | array shapes: `[]` (`simple = 1`, `flat_length = 0`, jsvalue.c:434-439); `[1]`; contiguous append 0..8 forcing the capacity doubling from 8 (jsrun.c:677-689); sparse write `a[100]=1` on a simple array ⇒ `jsR_unflattenarray` converts to a property tree (jsrun.c:545-566, 720-728); an already-unflattened array. | [ ] |\n| 90 | `jsR_setproperty` (`length`), `jsV_resizearray` | array `length` shapes: `a.length = 0` shrinking a simple array (`flat_length` truncated, jsrun.c:710-713); `a.length = 5` on a sparse array (`jsV_resizearray`, jsproperty.c:320-341 — both the iterator-based branch when `length > count*2` and the linear branch); `a.length = 1.5` → RangeError `\"invalid array length\"`; `a.length = -1` → same; `a.length = 4294967295` (2^32-1) → RangeError `\"array too large\"` because it exceeds `JS_ARRAYLIMIT` (jsrun.c:702-718). | [ ] |\n| 91 | `jsR_setarrayindex` | `a[i] = v` with `i + 1 > JS_ARRAYLIMIT` → RangeError `\"array too large\"` (jsrun.c:675-676). | [ ] |\n| 92 | `jsR_delindex` | delete the last element of a simple array (`k == flat_length-1` ⇒ just decrement, no unflatten) vs delete a middle element (falls through to `jsR_delproperty` and unflattens) (jsrun.c:925-933). | [ ] |\n| 93 | `js_isarrayindex` | index-string classification: `\"\"` → no; `\"0\"` → yes(0); `\"00\"`, `\"01\"` → no (leading zero); `\"123\"` → yes; `\"12a\"` → no; `\"9999999999\"` → no (`n >= INT_MAX/10` guard) (jsrun.c:509-532). | [ ] |\n| 94 | `js_newfunction`, `js_newscript` | JS function object: `js_dostring(J,\"function f(a,b){}\")` then inspect `f.length == 2` (`JS_READONLY|JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF`), `f.prototype.constructor === f` (jsvalue.c:456-472). `js_newscript` creates a `JS_CSCRIPT` object with **no** prototype and no `length`/`prototype` properties (jsvalue.c:474-480) — `js_iscallable` true, `js_typeof` → `\"object\"` (jsrun.c:248-250, 293). | [ ] |\n| 95 | `js_newcfunction`, `js_newcfunctionx` | C function shapes: `js_newcfunction(J, f, \"f\", 0)`, `(…, 1)`, `(…, 7)` — `length` drives the `jsR_callcfunction` padding of missing args with `undefined` (jsrun.c:1266-1285). `js_newcfunctionx(J,f,\"f\",1,data,fin)` with `data`/`finalize` non-NULL (finalizer runs at GC, jsgc.c:51-52; and on a throw during construction, jsvalue.c:486-490) vs both NULL. | [ ] |\n| 96 | `js_currentfunctiondata`, `js_currentfunction` | inside a cfunction created with `js_newcfunctionx(..., data, ...)`: `js_currentfunctiondata(J)` returns `data`; called with `BOT == 0` returns NULL (jsrun.c:211-216). `js_currentfunction` with `BOT > 0` copies `STACK[BOT-1]`, with `BOT == 0` pushes undefined (jsrun.c:201-209). | [ ] |\n| 97 | `js_newcconstructor` | `js_pushobject(J, proto); js_newcconstructor(J, callfn, ctorfn, \"C\", 1)` — sets both `u.c.function` and `u.c.constructor`, so `js_construct` takes the \"built-in constructor creates its own object\" path with a `null` this (jsrun.c:1346-1359). Contrast a plain `js_newcfunction` used with `new` (goes through the prototype/`newobj` path, jsrun.c:1361-1387). | [ ] |\n| 98 | `js_newuserdata`, `js_newuserdatax`, `js_isuserdata`, `js_touserdata` | userdata shapes: `js_pushobject(J,proto)`/`js_pushundefined(J)` then `js_newuserdata(J,\"Tag\",p,fin)` (has/put/del all NULL) vs `js_newuserdatax(J,\"Tag\",p,has,put,del,fin)` with each callback individually non-NULL and returning 0 vs 1 — drives the `JS_CUSERDATA` branches in `jsR_hasproperty` (jsrun.c:625-628), `jsR_setproperty` (jsrun.c:755-758), `jsR_defproperty` (jsrun.c:843-846) and `jsR_delproperty` (jsrun.c:906-909). Also `js_isuserdata`/`js_touserdata` with a matching vs mismatching tag (TypeError `\"not a Tag\"`, jsrun.c:266-272, 376-383). | [ ] |\n| 99 | `js_newregexp`, `js_toregexp`, `js_isregexp` | regexp shapes: `js_newregexp(J,\"a/b\",0)` (source escaped by `escaperegexp`, jsregexp.c:4-20), flags `0`, `JS_REGEXP_G`, `JS_REGEXP_I`, `JS_REGEXP_M`, and `G|I|M`; invalid pattern `\"(\"` → SyntaxError `\"regular expression: ...\"` (jsregexp.c:36-37); `js_toregexp` on a non-regexp → TypeError `\"not a regexp\"` (jsrun.c:368-374). | [ ] |\n| 100 | `jsB_new_Date`, `js_todate` | Date object shapes: `new Date()` (top==1, `Now()`), `new Date(0)`, `new Date(NaN)` (invalid), `new Date(\"2020-01-02T03:04:05.678Z\")`, `new Date({})` (`js_toprimitive` then number), `new Date(2020, 0)` (7-arg path with defaults `d=1,H=M=S=ms=0`), `new Date(99, 0)` (`y < 100 ⇒ y += 1900`), `new Date(8.64e15+1)` → `TimeClip` NaN (jsdate.c:228-235, 412-444). | [ ] |\n| 101 | `jsB_ErrorX`, `js_newerror`…`js_newurierror` | Error object shapes: `new Error()` (no `message` property — `js_isdefined(J,1)` false), `new Error(\"m\")`, `new TypeError(\"m\")`, plus the C entry points `js_newerror/js_newevalerror/js_newrangeerror/js_newreferenceerror/js_newsyntaxerror/js_newtypeerror/js_newurierror` (which always set `message` and `stackTrace` via `js_setproperty`) (jserror.c:63-107). Check `js_iserror` true and `stackTrace` presence with `tracetop - skip <= 0` (no trace ⇒ property omitted, jserror.c:6-11, 70-71). | [ ] |\n| 102 | `js_newarguments` | arguments object: `js_dostring(J, \"function f(){return arguments} f()\")` (n==0), `f(1)`, `f(1,2,3)` — `JS_CARGUMENTS` class with `Object_prototype`, `length` = actual arg count, indices set via `js_setindex` (jsvalue.c:429-432, jsrun.c:1203-1217). `Op_toString` → `\"[object Arguments]\"` (jsobject.c:41). | [ ] |\n| 103 | `js_pushiterator`, `jsV_newiterator`, `jsV_nextiterator`, `js_nextiterator` | `js_pushiterator(J, idx, 0)` (`own = 0` ⇒ `itflatten` walks the whole prototype chain and suppresses shadowed names, jsproperty.c:266-274, 287) vs `own = 1` (own enumerable properties only, jsproperty.c:282-285). Targets: empty object, object with DONTENUM properties, object shadowing a prototype property, a simple array (`iter.n = flat_length`, jsproperty.c:294-295), a `JS_CSTRING` (`iter.n = u.s.length`, jsproperty.c:291-292). Then `js_nextiterator` until NULL, including the case where a property was deleted mid-iteration (`jsV_getproperty` recheck, jsproperty.c:312-313), and `js_nextiterator` on a non-iterator → TypeError `\"not an iterator\"` (jsproperty.c:302-303). | [ ] |\n| 104 | `jsV_toobject`, `js_toobject` | `ToObject` for each primitive: undefined/null → TypeError; string (shrstr/litstr/memstr each) → `JS_CSTRING`; boolean → `JS_CBOOLEAN`; number → `JS_CNUMBER`; object → identity. Note the stack slot is *mutated in place* to the new object (jsvalue.c:410-411) — verify via `js_getproperty(J, idx, \"x\")` followed by `js_type(J, idx)`. | [ ] |\n| 105 | `js_instanceof` | `js_dostring` shapes: `({}) instanceof Object` (true), `1 instanceof Object` (non-object LHS → false, jsvalue.c:581-582), `({}) instanceof 1` → TypeError `\"instanceof: invalid operand\"`, and a function whose `prototype` is a primitive → TypeError `\"instanceof: 'prototype' property is not an object\"` (jsvalue.c:574-598). Also `Fp_bind`'s prototype reuse (jsfunction.c:194-196). | [ ] |\n| 106 | `js_concat` | `js_pushnumber(J,1); js_pushnumber(J,2); js_concat(J)` → 3 (numeric branch) vs `js_pushstring(J,\"a\"); js_pushnumber(J,1); js_concat(J)` → `\"a1\"` (string branch, allocates and frees a temp buffer with a `js_try` guard, jsvalue.c:600-627). Also both operands objects with `valueOf`/`toString` (`JS_HNONE` hint, jsvalue.c:602-603). | [ ] |\n| 107 | `js_compare`, `js_equal`, `js_strictequal` | `js_compare` with two strings (`strcmp`), with NaN on either side (`*okay = 0`, jsvalue.c:640-641), and mixed types. `js_equal` covering each branch of jsvalue.c:646-691: string/string, same-type undefined/null/number/boolean/object, null vs undefined, number vs string, boolean coercion (`goto retry`), object vs primitive (`jsV_toprimitive` then retry). `js_strictequal` for the same shapes (no coercion, jsvalue.c:693-708). | [ ] |\n| 108 | `js_repr`, `js_torepr`, `js_tryrepr` | `js_repr(J, idx)` for one value of every shape so each `reprvalue` case fires (jsrepr.c:151-240): undefined, null, bool, `-0`, string with `\"`/`\\`/`\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t`/`\\x01`/U+00E9/U+1F600, plain object, nested/cyclic object (`{}` short-circuit, jsrepr.c:84-92), array (incl. cyclic ⇒ `[]`), JS function, C function, `new Boolean/Number/String`, regexp with flags, Date, Error with and without `mes