{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_x6fghnay2hf3lfwrnzol5z6p74v7ftngzjg5skubq7obi2zg2thq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01YZ7aycT5TpPEnRxm5Y3wzD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jqRrno/translation/CONFIGS_part2.md","content":"# CONFIGS — Part 2: regexp engine\n\nScope: `c_src/src/regexp.c`, `c_src/src/regexp.h`, `c_src/src/jsregexp.c`.\n\nConstants read from the source: `REPINF = 255` (regexp.c:16), `REG_MAXPROG = 32<<10`\n(regexp.c:18), `REG_MAXREC = 4096` (regexp.c:21), `REG_MAXSPAN = 64` (regexp.c:24),\n`REG_MAXCLASS = 128` (regexp.c:27), `REG_MAXSUB = 16` (regexp.h:35),\n`REG_ICASE = 1`, `REG_NEWLINE = 2`, `REG_NOTBOL = 4` (regexp.h:22-29),\n`JS_REGEXP_G = 1`, `JS_REGEXP_I = 2`, `JS_REGEXP_M = 4` (include/mujs.h:82-84).\n\nEngine semantics derived from the source, used to pick the expected results below:\n\n* `js_regexec` returns `0` = match, `1` = no match, `-1` = recursion limit hit\n  (regexp.c:1080-1081, 1222, 1075-1076).\n* Every compiled program is prefixed with `split / anynl / jump` (regexp.c:967-972) where\n  `split->x = split+3` is tried *before* `split->y`, so the search is **leftmost** and the\n  start position advances one *rune* at a time, crossing newlines (`I_ANYNL`, regexp.c:1115-1119).\n* `I_SPLIT` recurses into `pc->x` first and returns on the first success (regexp.c:1085-1095):\n  the engine is **leftmost-first (backtracking / Perl)**, *not* POSIX leftmost-longest.\n* `REG_ICASE` acts at compile time on `I_CHAR` (regexp.c:792) and at run time on\n  `I_CHAR` / `I_CCLASS` / `I_NCCLASS` / `I_REF` (regexp.c:1130, 1139, 1151, 1162).\n* `REG_NEWLINE` affects **only** `I_BOL` / `I_EOL` (regexp.c:1179, 1191); there is no dotall\n  option, `I_ANY` always rejects newlines (regexp.c:1123).\n* `REG_NOTBOL` affects **only** `I_BOL` (regexp.c:1175).\n* `eflags` are OR-ed into `prog->flags` at exec time (regexp.c:1239), so `REG_ICASE` and\n  `REG_NEWLINE` are also *accepted* (with partial effect) as exec flags.\n* Group 0 is the whole match, so a pattern may contain at most **15** capturing groups\n  (`g->nsub` starts at 1, regexp.c:931; `die` at regexp.c:551-552).\n* There are no octal escapes and no dotall/sticky/unicode flags; `^` and `$` are recognised\n  only at `parserep` level (regexp.c:588-589) so they cannot be quantified.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n\n## A. Compile flags (`cflags`) on `js_regcomp` / `js_regcompx`\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=0, eflags=0, pattern `abc`; subject `\"xabcx\"` → match at byte 1; subject `\"abd\"` → no match. Baseline: `g->prog->flags = cflags` (regexp.c:935), wrapper regexp.c:1007 | [ ] |\n| 2 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, pattern `abc`; subject `\"ABC\"` → match (operand canonicalised at compile time regexp.c:792, input at run time regexp.c:1130-1131); subject `\"abd\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 3 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, pattern `[a-z]+`; subject `\"ABC\"` → match (`incclasscanon`, regexp.c:1139-1141, 1041-1049). Also pattern `[^a]` with subject `\"A\"` → **no** match (negation folds too, regexp.c:1151-1153), subject `\"b\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 4 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, pattern `(a)\\1`; subject `\"aA\"` → match via `strncmpcanon` (regexp.c:1162-1164, 1051-1065); the same pattern/subject with cflags=0 → no match (plain `strncmp`, regexp.c:1166) | [ ] |\n| 5 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, non-ASCII: pattern `É`, subject `\"é\"` (UTF-8 C3 A9) → match via `canon`/`toupperrune` (regexp.c:73-79); pattern `ſ` (U+017F), subject `\"s\"`/`\"S\"` → **no** match because `canon` leaves runes ≥128 whose uppercase is <128 unfolded (regexp.c:76-77) | [ ] |\n| 6 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), eflags=0, pattern `^b`; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at 2 (regexp.c:1179-1183); identical call with cflags=0 → no match | [ ] |\n| 7 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), eflags=0, pattern `a$`; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at 0 (regexp.c:1191-1195); with cflags=0 → no match. Also `\\r` as terminator: pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\rb\"` → match (`isnewline` accepts 0xD, regexp.c:1021) | [ ] |\n| 8 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), eflags=0, pattern `a.b`; subject `\"a\\nb\"` → **no** match — `REG_NEWLINE` does not turn on dotall (regexp.c:1120-1126); subject `\"axb\"` → match. Also pattern `a.b` with subject `\"a\u2028b\"` → no match, since `I_ANY` sees the decoded rune and `isnewline` covers U+2028 (regexp.c:1019-1022) | [ ] |\n| 9 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE\\|REG_NEWLINE(3), eflags=0, pattern `^abc$`; subject `\"x\\nABC\\ny\"` → match; subject `\"x\\nABD\\ny\"` → no match. Both bits sit in `prog->flags` and are consulted independently (regexp.c:935, 1130/1179/1191) | [ ] |\n\n## B. JS-level flags (`JS_REGEXP_G/I/M`) and their mapping onto `cflags` (`jsregexp.c`)\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 10 | js_newregexp | flags=0 (no g/i/m), pattern `abc` → `opts = 0` (jsregexp.c:32-34) and `js_regcompx(J->alloc, J->actx, \"abc\", 0, &error)` (jsregexp.c:36); `/abc/.exec(\"abc\")` → match | [ ] |\n| 11 | js_newregexp + js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=JS_REGEXP_G(1), pattern `a` → `opts = 0`; G only enables `re->last` bookkeeping (jsregexp.c:62-73, 88-89). Subject `\"aa\"`: first exec → index 0 / `last=1`; second exec → index 1 / `last=2`; third → `null` and `last=0` (jsregexp.c:93-96) | [ ] |\n| 12 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_I(2), pattern `abc` → `opts = REG_ICASE` (jsregexp.c:33); subject `\"ABC\"` → match; subject `\"abd\"` → null | [ ] |\n| 13 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_M(4), pattern `^b` → `opts = REG_NEWLINE` (jsregexp.c:34); subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at index 2; subject `\"ab\"` → null | [ ] |\n| 14 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_I (3) → `opts = REG_ICASE`; pattern `a`, subject `\"xA\"` → match at index 1, `last=2` | [ ] |\n| 15 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_M (5) → `opts = REG_NEWLINE`; pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at index 2 | [ ] |\n| 16 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_I\\|JS_REGEXP_M (6) → `opts = REG_ICASE\\|REG_NEWLINE` (3); pattern `^b$`, subject `\"a\\nB\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 17 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_I\\|JS_REGEXP_M (7) → `opts = 3` plus lastIndex tracking; pattern `^b$`, subject `\"a\\nB\\nb\"` → two successive matches then null | [ ] |\n| 18 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G with `re->last == 0`: pattern `a`, subject `\"ab\"` → `opts = 0` and `haystack == text` (jsregexp.c:68 branch not taken) | [ ] |\n| 19 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G, no M, `re->last = 2`: pattern `^a`, subject `\"xxab\"` → `haystack = text+2` and `opts \\|= REG_NOTBOL` (jsregexp.c:69-72) → **no** match although `haystack[0] == 'a'` | [ ] |\n| 20 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G\\|M, `re->last = 2`, `haystack[-1] == '\\n'`: pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → `REG_NOTBOL` **not** set (jsregexp.c:70) → match at index 2 | [ ] |\n| 21 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G\\|M, `re->last = 2`, `haystack[-1] != '\\n'`: pattern `^c`, subject `\"abc\"` → `REG_NOTBOL` **is** set (jsregexp.c:70-71) → null | [ ] |\n| 22 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G with a stale `re->last`: `re->last = 5`, subject `\"abc\"` → `last > strlen` so `last` is reset to 0 and `null` returned without calling `js_regexec` (jsregexp.c:63-67). Boundary case `re->last = 3 == strlen` is *not* `>`, so pattern `x*` still execs on the empty tail with `REG_NOTBOL` | [ ] |\n| 23 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `(b)(c)`, subject `\"abc\"` → array of `m.nsub` (=3) elements (jsregexp.c:84-87) with `nsub` taken from `prog->nsub` (regexp.c:953, 1235); an unset group (pattern `(a)\\|(b)`, subject `\"b\"`) yields `\"\"` because `sp == ep == NULL` (regexp.c:1236-1237, jsregexp.c:85) | [ ] |\n| 24 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `b`, non-ASCII subject `\"ééb\"` → `index` is the **rune** index 2 via `js_utfptrtoidx` (jsregexp.c:82), not the byte offset 4 | [ ] |\n| 25 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec / Rp_test | flags=G, pattern `a`, subject `\"xaxa\"` — `Rp_test` accumulates `re->last + (ep - text)` on the *already advanced* pointer (jsregexp.c:128-129) whereas `exec` stores the absolute `ep - text` (jsregexp.c:89); run both to confirm identical lastIndex progression. Also flags=G with zero-width pattern `a*` on `\"b\"` → match of length 0 and `last` stays 0 (no progress) | [ ] |\n| 26 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp()` (pattern undefined) and `new RegExp(\"\")` (zero length) both compile the substitute pattern `(?:)` with flags=0 (jsregexp.c:154-156, 162-163) → matches empty at index 0 for any subject | [ ] |\n| 27 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp(\"a/b\")` with `is_clone == 0` → `source` is `escaperegexp`'d to `a\\/b` while the compiled pattern stays `a/b` (jsregexp.c:4-21, 41); subject `\"a/b\"` → match. Clone path `new RegExp(/a\\/b/g)`: `is_clone == 1`, `pattern = old->source`, `source = js_strdup`, flags copied (jsregexp.c:147-153) — recompiling `a\\/b` still yields `/` because `/` is neither in `ESCAPES` nor a letter (regexp.c:167-171) | [ ] |\n| 28 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | flags-string configuration: `\"\"` → 0, `\"g\"` → 1, `\"i\"` → 2, `\"m\"` → 4, `\"gim\"` → 7 (jsregexp.c:165-181); `Rp_toString` renders them back in fixed `g,i,m` order (jsregexp.c:209-211) | [ ] |\n| 29 | jsB_initregexp | prototype install: `RegExp.prototype.{toString,test,exec}` plus the `RegExp` constructor/`new` pair with length 1, defined global as DONTENUM (jsregexp.c:224-234). The prototype object itself carries a prog compiled from `\"(?:)\"` with cflags=0 and `errorp == NULL` (jsbuiltin.c:205) | [ ] |\n| 30 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G against a subject longer than 65535 bytes with a match past offset 65535 → `re->last` is `unsigned short` (jsi.h:367) and truncates; exercise a >64 KiB subject to pin the wrap behaviour | [ ] |\n\n## C. Exec flags (`eflags` on `js_regexec`)\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 31 | js_regexec | eflags=0, cflags=0, pattern `^a`, subject `\"abc\"` → match at 0 (first branch of `I_BOL`, regexp.c:1175) | [ ] |\n| 32 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NOTBOL(4), cflags=0, pattern `^a`, subject `\"abc\"` → **no** match (`!(flags & REG_NOTBOL)` fails and there is no `REG_NEWLINE` fallback, regexp.c:1175-1185) | [ ] |\n| 33 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NOTBOL(4), cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at 2 through the newline branch only (regexp.c:1179-1184) | [ ] |\n| 34 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NOTBOL(4) on patterns with no `I_BOL`: pattern `abc` on `\"abc\"` → match; pattern `a$` on `\"a\"` → match; pattern `\\ba` on `\"abc\"` → match (`I_EOL`/`I_WORD` never read the flag, regexp.c:1186-1204) | [ ] |\n| 35 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_ICASE(1) on a prog compiled with cflags=0 (flags are OR-ed at regexp.c:1239): pattern `A` with subject `\"a\"` → match (run-time `canon`, regexp.c:1130); pattern `a` with subject `\"A\"` → **no** match, because the `I_CHAR` operand was never canonicalised at regexp.c:792 | [ ] |\n| 36 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NEWLINE(2) on a prog compiled with cflags=0: pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match; multiline can be switched on per-exec without recompiling (regexp.c:1239, 1179) | [ ] |\n| 37 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_ICASE\\|REG_NEWLINE\\|REG_NOTBOL(7) simultaneously, cflags=0, pattern `^b$`, subject `\"a\\nB\"` → match | [ ] |\n| 38 | js_regexec | `sub == NULL`: pattern `(a)(b)`, cflags=0, eflags=0, subject `\"ab\"` → the internal `scratch` Resub is used and captures are dropped, return value still 0 (regexp.c:1229-1233) | [ ] |\n| 39 | js_regexec | one `Reprog` reused across consecutive calls with different subjects and different eflags — all 16 `sub[]` slots are re-cleared and `sub->nsub` re-stamped from `prog->nsub` on every call (regexp.c:1235-1237); `js_regexec` performs no allocation | [ ] |\n\n## D. Pattern shapes — literals and `.`\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 40 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | single literal rune and literal concatenation: pattern `a` on `\"a\"` → match, on `\"b\"` → no match; pattern `abc` on `\"zabcz\"` → match at 1, on `\"acb\"` → no match. cflags=0, eflags=0. `L_CHAR`→`P_CHAR`→`I_CHAR` (regexp.c:440, 520-525, 790-793) spliced by the right-leaning `P_CAT` chain (regexp.c:607-624, 701-704) | [ ] |\n| 41 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | non-ASCII literal rune: pattern `é` (UTF-8 C3 A9), cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"xéx\"` → match; subject `\"xex\"` → no match. Rune decoded by `chartorune` at compile (regexp.c:125) and match (regexp.c:1129) time | [ ] |\n| 42 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | `.` any-but-newline: pattern `a.c`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"abc\"` → match; subject `\"a\\nc\"` → no match (`P_ANY`/`I_ANY`, regexp.c:547-548, 787-789, 1120-1126). Also pattern `a.` on `\"a\"` → no match (`if (!*sp) return 1`, regexp.c:1121) | [ ] |\n| 43 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | `.` consumes a whole multi-byte rune: pattern `^.$`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"é\"` (2 bytes) → match; subject `\"éé\"` → no match (regexp.c:1122) | [ ] |\n\n## E. Pattern shapes — character classes\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 44 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | positive class of singletons: pattern `[abc]`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"c\"` → match; subject `\"d\"` → no match (`lexclass`→`L_CCLASS`→`P_CCLASS`/`I_CCLASS`, regexp.c:305-385, 526-531, 794-797, 1136-1147) | [ ] |\n| 45 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | negated class: pattern `[^abc]`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"d\"` → match; subject `\"b\"` → no match (`^` detected at regexp.c:314-317; `I_NCCLASS` regexp.c:798-801, 1148-1159) | [ ] |\n| 46 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | class range: pattern `[a-f]`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"c\"` → match; subject `\"g\"` → no match (dash path regexp.c:326-337 + 361-368, `addrange` regexp.c:218-256). Non-ASCII range `[à-ÿ]` on `\"é\"` → match, on `\"a\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 47 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | overlapping / adjacent ranges coalesced by `addrange`: pattern `[a-cb-e0-9]`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"d\"` → match; subject `\"z\"` → no match (the four extend/swallow branches, regexp.c:229-249) | [ ] |\n| 48 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | negated range: pattern `[^0-9]`, cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"x\"` → match; subject `\"7\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 49 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | `-` as a literal in all three positions: pattern `[-a]` (leading, regexp.c:334-337), pattern `[a-]` (trailing flush, regexp.c:378-382), pattern `[a--]` (`save,'-'` path, regexp.c:327-330); cflags=0, eflags=0; subject `\"-\"` → match for all three; subject `\"b\"` → no match | [ ] |\n| 50 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | positive class escapes inside a class: pattern `[\\d]` on `\"5\"` → match / on `\"x\"` → no match; `[\\s]` on `\"\\t\"`/`\" \"` → match / on `\"a\"` → no match; `[\\w]` on `\"_\"` → match / on `\"-\"` → no match. cflags=0, eflags=0 (regexp.c:338-351 with 345/346/347 → 258-261, 269-276, 288-294) | [ ] |\n| 51 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | negative class escapes inside a positive class (they expand to complement *spans*, the class itself stays positive): `[\\D]` 