swhite(...) \\|\\| lexchar == '\\n'`, a DIFFERENT predicate combination from `jsY_lex`'s; source = `\"\"` → EOF → 0; and each structural token `, : [ ] { }` (jslex.c:840-855, 873-874) | [ ] |\n178\t| 102 | `jsY_lexjson` → `lexjsonnumber` | `\"0\"` (the `lexchar == '0'` single-step branch) vs `\"10\"` (the `'1'..'9'` digit-run branch) vs `\"-0\"` (leading minus, also the alternate entry condition at jslex.c:846); `\"1.25\"` (fraction); `\"1e5\"` / `\"1E-5\"` / `\"1e+5\"` (exponent with each sign form) → then `js_strtod(s, NULL)` (jslex.c:747-782) | [ ] |\n179\t| 103 | `jsY_lexjson` → `lexjsonstring` / `lexjsonescape` | `\"\\\"a\\\"\"` → plain characters, with c = 0x20 (space) the lowest byte NOT rejected by `lexchar < 32`; all eight simple escapes `\\\" \\\\ \\/ \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t` (note `\\/` exists only in the JSON escaper, while `\\'`, `\\v`, `\\0` and `\\x` do not); `\"\\\"\\\\u00e9\\\"\"` → the 4-hex-digit branch; and the literals `\"true\"`, `\"false\"`, `\"null\"` via their `jsY_expect` chains (jslex.c:784-836, 861-871) | [ ] |\n180\t\n181\t## jsstring.c / jsrun.c — low-level string helpers\n182\t\n183\t| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n184\t|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n185\t| 104 | `js_isarrayindex` | name = `\"\"` → the explicit empty-string check → 0 (jsrun.c:512-514) | [ ] |\n186\t| 105 | `js_isarrayindex` | name = `\"0\"` → `*idx = 0`, returns 1; name = `\"01\"` and `\"00\"` → leading-zero rejection (`p[0]=='0'` and `p[1]!=0`) → 0. Then name = `\"123\"` → 1 with `*idx = 123`; name ∈ {`\"12a\"`, `\"1.5\"`, `\"-1\"`, `\"1 \"`} → a non-digit anywhere → 0 (jsrun.c:516-531) | [ ] |\n187\t| 106 | `js_isarrayindex` | the `n >= INT_MAX / 10` guard: name = `\"214748364\"` is ACCEPTED (before the last digit `n == 21474836 < 214748364`) while name = `\"2147483647\"` (INT_MAX itself) and name = `\"9999999999999999999\"` are REJECTED (jsrun.c:524-526) | [ ] |\n188\t| 107 | `js_utflen` | string = `\"\"` → the `c == 0` early return with n = 0; string = `\"abc\"` → 3 via the `c < Runeself` branch; string = `\"\\xC2\\xA2\\xE2\\x82\\xAC\"` (2-byte + 3-byte, each `< 0x10000`) → 2; string = `\"\\xF0\\x9F\\x98\\x80\"` (rune ≥ 0x10000) → 2, i.e. UTF-16 length; string = `\"a\\xC0\\x80b\"` (embedded overlong-NUL) → `chartorune` yields rune 0 with length 2 and the scan does NOT terminate → 3 (jsstring.c:48-68; utf.c:58-61) | [ ] |\n189\t| 108 | `js_utfptrtoidx` | s == p → 0 (loop never runs); s = `\"abcdef\"`, p = s+3 → 3 (ASCII branch); s = `\"\\xF0\\x9F\\x98\\x80x\"`, p = s+4 → 2 (astral rune counts 2); s = `\"\\xE2\\x82\\xACx\"`, p = s+1 (p points INTO a multi-byte sequence) → the `s < p` loop overshoots and returns 1, i.e. unaligned p is not guarded (jsstring.c:70-88) | [ ] |\n190\t| 109 | `js_runeat` | s = `\"abc\"`, i = 0 → `'a'`; s = `\"abc\"`, i = 3 → walks off the end, `rune == 0` → EOF; s = `\"\"`, i = 0 → immediate EOF; s = `\"abc\"`, i = -1 → `while (i >= 0)` never runs so the initialiser `rune = EOF` is returned (jsstring.c:20-29, 45) | [ ] |\n191\t| 110 | `js_runeat` | multi-byte indexing (the `rune >= Runeself` half of the walk): s = `\"\\xE2\\x82\\xAC\"`, i = 0 → 3-byte rune `< 0x10000`, single index step → 0x20AC; s = `\"\\xF0\\x9F\\x98\\x80\"`, i = 0 → rune ≥ 0x10000 so `i` drops by 2 to -2 → high surrogate `0xd800 + ((rune-0x10000)>>10)`; the same string with i = 1 → `i` lands at -1 → low surrogate `0xdc00 + ((rune-0x10000)&0x3ff)`; s = `\"a\\xF0\\x9F\\x98\\x80\"`, i = 2 → ASCII prefix then the high surrogate (jsstring.c:30-44) | [ ] |\n192\t| 111 | `jsV_newmemstring` | n = 3 with s = `\"abc\"` → allocates `soffsetof(js_String, p) + 4`, copies 3 bytes, appends a NUL, links into `J->gcstr` and bumps `gccounter`; n = 1 with s = `\"5\"` (minimum non-empty payload); n = 0 with s = `\"\"` → a 1-byte payload holding only the terminator (`memcpy` of length 0); n = 5 with s = `\"ab\\0cd\"` → all five bytes including the interior NUL are copied, since `n` is supplied by the caller and never re-derived with `strlen` (jsrun.c:94-103) | [ ] |\n193\t\n194\t\n195\t# Part 2 — regexp engine (regexp.c, jsregexp.c)\n196\t\n197\t\n198\tScope: `c_src/src/regexp.c`, `c_src/src/regexp.h`, `c_src/src/jsregexp.c`.\n199\t\n200\tConstants read from the source: `REPINF = 255` (regexp.c:16), `REG_MAXPROG = 32<<10`\n201\t(regexp.c:18), `REG_MAXREC = 4096` (regexp.c:21), `REG_MAXSPAN = 64` (regexp.c:24),\n202\t`REG_MAXCLASS = 128` (regexp.c:27), `REG_MAXSUB = 16` (regexp.h:35),\n203\t`REG_ICASE = 1`, `REG_NEWLINE = 2`, `REG_NOTBOL = 4` (regexp.h:22-29),\n204\t`JS_REGEXP_G = 1`, `JS_REGEXP_I = 2`, `JS_REGEXP_M = 4` (include/mujs.h:82-84).\n205\t\n206\tEngine semantics derived from the source, used to pick the expected results below:\n207\t\n208\t* `js_regexec` returns `0` = match, `1` = no match, `-1` = recursion limit hit\n209\t  (regexp.c:1080-1081, 1222, 1075-1076).\n210\t* Every compiled program is prefixed with `split / anynl / jump` (regexp.c:967-972) where\n211\t  `split->x = split+3` is tried *before* `split->y`, so the search is **leftmost** and the\n212\t  start position advances one *rune* at a time, crossing newlines (`I_ANYNL`, regexp.c:1115-1119).\n213\t* `I_SPLIT` recurses into `pc->x` first and returns on the first success (regexp.c:1085-1095):\n214\t  the engine is **leftmost-first (backtracking / Perl)**, *not* POSIX leftmost-longest.\n215\t* `REG_ICASE` acts at compile time on `I_CHAR` (regexp.c:792) and at run time on\n216\t  `I_CHAR` / `I_CCLASS` / `I_NCCLASS` / `I_REF` (regexp.c:1130, 1139, 1151, 1162).\n217\t* `REG_NEWLINE` affects **only** `I_BOL` / `I_EOL` (regexp.c:1179, 1191); there is no dotall\n218\t  option, `I_ANY` always rejects newlines (regexp.c:1123).\n219\t* `REG_NOTBOL` affects **only** `I_BOL` (regexp.c:1175).\n220\t* `eflags` are OR-ed into `prog->flags` at exec time (regexp.c:1239), so `REG_ICASE` and\n221\t  `REG_NEWLINE` are also *accepted* (with partial effect) as exec flags.\n222\t* Group 0 is the whole match, so a pattern may contain at most **15** capturing groups\n223\t  (`g->nsub` starts at 1, regexp.c:931; `die` at regexp.c:551-552).\n224\t* There are no octal escapes and no dotall/sticky/unicode flags; `^` and `$` are recognised\n225\t  only at `parserep` level (regexp.c:588-589) and therefore cannot be quantified.\n226\t\n227\t| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n228\t|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n229\t\n230\t## A. Compile flags (`cflags`) on `js_regcomp` / `js_regcompx`\n231\t\n232\t| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n233\t|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n234\t| 112 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=0, eflags=0, pattern `abc`; subject `\"xabcx\"` → match at byte 1; subject `\"abd\"` → no match. Baseline: `g->prog->flags = cflags` (regexp.c:935), wrapper regexp.c:1007 | [ ] |\n235\t| 113 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, pattern `abc`; subject `\"ABC\"` → match (operand canonicalised at compile time regexp.c:792, input at run time regexp.c:1130-1131); subject `\"abd\"` → no match | [ ] |\n236\t| 114 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, pattern `[a-z]+`; subject `\"ABC\"` → match (`incclasscanon`, regexp.c:1139-1141, 1041-1049). Also pattern `[^a]` with subject `\"A\"` → **no** match (negation folds too, regexp.c:1151-1153), subject `\"b\"` → match | [ ] |\n237\t| 115 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, pattern `(a)\\1`; subject `\"aA\"` → match via `strncmpcanon` (regexp.c:1162-1164, 1051-1065); the same pattern/subject with cflags=0 → no match (plain `strncmp`, regexp.c:1166) | [ ] |\n238\t| 116 | js_regcomp + js_regexec | cflags=REG_ICASE(1), eflags=0, non-ASCII: pattern `É`, subject `\"é\"` (UTF-8 C3 A9) → match via `canon`/`toupperrune` (regexp.c:73-79); pattern `ſ` (U+017F), subject `\"s\"`/`\"S\"` → **no