-96) | [ ] |\n250\t| 123 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_I(2), pattern `abc` → `opts = REG_ICASE` (jsregexp.c:33); subject `\"ABC\"` → match; subject `\"abd\"` → null | [ ] |\n251\t| 124 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_M(4), pattern `^b` → `opts = REG_NEWLINE` (jsregexp.c:34); subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at index 2; subject `\"ab\"` → null | [ ] |\n252\t| 125 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_I (3) → `opts = REG_ICASE`; pattern `a`, subject `\"xA\"` → match at index 1 with `last=2` | [ ] |\n253\t| 126 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_M (5) → `opts = REG_NEWLINE`; pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at index 2 | [ ] |\n254\t| 127 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_I\\|JS_REGEXP_M (6) → `opts = REG_ICASE\\|REG_NEWLINE` (3); pattern `^b$`, subject `\"a\\nB\"` → match | [ ] |\n255\t| 128 | js_newregexp | flags=JS_REGEXP_G\\|JS_REGEXP_I\\|JS_REGEXP_M (7) → `opts = 3` plus lastIndex tracking; pattern `^b$`, subject `\"a\\nB\\nb\"` → two successive matches then null | [ ] |\n256\t| 129 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G with `re->last == 0`: pattern `a`, subject `\"ab\"` → `opts = 0`, `haystack == text` (jsregexp.c:68 branch not taken) | [ ] |\n257\t| 130 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G, no M, `re->last = 2`: pattern `^a`, subject `\"xxab\"` → `haystack = text+2` and `opts \\|= REG_NOTBOL` (jsregexp.c:69-72) → **no** match although `haystack[0] == 'a'` | [ ] |\n258\t| 131 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G\\|M, `re->last = 2`, `haystack[-1] == '\\n'`: pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → `REG_NOTBOL` **not** set (jsregexp.c:70) → match at index 2 | [ ] |\n259\t| 132 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G\\|M, `re->last = 2`, `haystack[-1] != '\\n'`: pattern `^c`, subject `\"abc\"` → `REG_NOTBOL` **is** set (jsregexp.c:70-71) → null | [ ] |\n260\t| 133 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G with a stale `re->last`: `last = 5`, subject `\"abc\"` → `last > strlen` so `last` is zeroed and `null` returned without ever calling `js_regexec` (jsregexp.c:63-67). Boundary `last = 3 == strlen` is not `>`, so pattern `x*` still execs on the empty tail with `REG_NOTBOL` set | [ ] |\n261\t| 134 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `(b)(c)`, subject `\"abc\"` → array of `m.nsub` (=3) elements (jsregexp.c:84-87) with `nsub` taken from `prog->nsub` (regexp.c:953, 1235); an unset group (pattern `(a)\\|(b)`, subject `\"b\"`) yields `\"\"` because `sp == ep == NULL` (regexp.c:1236-1237, jsregexp.c:85) | [ ] |\n262\t| 135 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=0, pattern `b`, non-ASCII subject `\"ééb\"` → `index` is the **rune** index 2 via `js_utfptrtoidx` (jsregexp.c:82), not the byte offset 4 | [ ] |\n263\t| 136 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec / Rp_test | flags=G, pattern `a`, subject `\"xaxa\"` — `Rp_test` accumulates `re->last + (ep - text)` on the *already advanced* pointer (jsregexp.c:128-129) whereas `exec` stores the absolute `ep - text` (jsregexp.c:89); run both to confirm identical lastIndex progression. Also flags=G with the zero-width pattern `a*` on `\"b\"` → match of length 0 and `last` stays 0 (no progress) | [ ] |\n264\t| 137 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp()` (pattern undefined) and `new RegExp(\"\")` (zero length) both compile the substitute pattern `(?:)` with flags=0 (jsregexp.c:154-156, 162-163) → zero-length match at index 0 for any subject | [ ] |\n265\t| 138 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | `new RegExp(\"a/b\")` with `is_clone == 0` → `source` is `escaperegexp`'d to `a\\/b` while the compiled pattern stays `a/b` (jsregexp.c:4-21, 41); subject `\"a/b\"` → match. Clone path `new RegExp(/a\\/b/g)`: `is_clone == 1`, `pattern = old->source`, `source = js_strdup`, flags copied (jsregexp.c:147-153) — recompiling `a\\/b` still yields `/`, since `/` is neither in `ESCAPES` nor a letter (regexp.c:167-171) | [ ] |\n266\t| 139 | js_newregexpx (jsB_new_RegExp) | flags-string configuration: `\"\"` → 0, `\"g\"` → 1, `\"i\"` → 2, `\"m\"` → 4, `\"gim\"` → 7 (jsregexp.c:165-181); `Rp_toString` renders them back in fixed `g,i,m` order (jsregexp.c:209-211) | [ ] |\n267\t| 140 | jsB_initregexp | prototype install: `RegExp.prototype.{toString,test,exec}` plus the `RegExp` constructor/`new` pair with length 1, defined as a DONTENUM global (jsregexp.c:224-234). The prototype object itself carries a prog compiled from `\"(?:)\"` with cflags=0 and `errorp == NULL` (jsbuiltin.c:205) | [ ] |\n268\t| 141 | js_RegExp_prototype_exec | flags=G on a subject longer than 65535 bytes with a match past offset 65535 → `re->last` is `unsigned short` (jsi.h:367) and truncates; exercise a >64 KiB subject to pin the wrap behaviour | [ ] |\n269\t\n270\t## C. Exec flags (`eflags` on `js_regexec`)\n271\t\n272\t| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n273\t|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n274\t| 142 | js_regexec | eflags=0, cflags=0, pattern `^a`, subject `\"abc\"` → match at 0 (first branch of `I_BOL`, regexp.c:1175) | [ ] |\n275\t| 143 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NOTBOL(4), cflags=0, pattern `^a`, subject `\"abc\"` → **no** match (`!(flags & REG_NOTBOL)` fails and there is no `REG_NEWLINE` fallback, regexp.c:1175-1185) | [ ] |\n276\t| 144 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NOTBOL(4), cflags=REG_NEWLINE(2), pattern `^b`, subject `\"a\\nb\"` → match at 2 through the newline branch only (regexp.c:1179-1184) | [ ] |\n277\t| 145 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NOTBOL(4) on patterns with no `I_BOL`: pattern `abc` on `\"abc\"` → match; pattern `a$` on `\"a\"` → match; pattern `\\ba` on `\"abc\"` → match — `I_EOL`/`I_WORD` never read the flag (regexp.c:1186-1204) | [ ] |\n278\t| 146 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_ICASE(1) on a prog compiled with cflags=0 (flags OR-ed at regexp.c:1239): pattern `A` with subject `\"a\"` → match (run-time `canon`, regexp.c:1130); pattern `a` with subject `\"A\"` → **no** match, because the `I_CHAR` operand was never canonicalised at regexp.c:792 | [ ] |\n279\t| 147 | js_regexec | eflags=REG_NEWLINE(2) on a