harCode(10)` or `new RegExp(String.fromCharCode(10))` instead.\n- The harness compares, between the C and Rust builds: the `js_ploadstring` return\n  code, the `js_pcall` return code, `js_type`, `js_typeof`, the stack depth, and the\n  STRINGIFIED result-or-error. So the exact error class and message text are compared.\n\nFor files 1-4 (the `errors_*` ones) there is an ADDITIONAL HARD REQUIREMENT enforced\nby a test called `error_corpora_actually_error_in_c`:\n  **EVERY snippet in errors_lexer.txt, errors_parser_compiler.txt,\n  errors_runtime.txt and errors_builtins.txt MUST actually produce an error in the C\n  build** (a non-zero `js_ploadstring` or `js_pcall` return code) in at least one of\n  the two modes (flags=0 or flags=JS_STRICT).\n  Therefore: do NOT wrap these snippets in try/catch (that would swallow the error).\n  Write the bare failing construct, e.g. the line `var 1x = 2` or the line `null.foo`.\n  VERIFY THIS YOURSELF (see \"How to verify\" below) and remove or fix any snippet\n  that does not error.\n\nCONTENT REQUIREMENTS — walk ERRORS.md and the C source, one snippet per distinct row:\n\n`errors_lexer.txt` — ERRORS.md section 12 (`jslex.c`, rows ~251-276): unterminated\n  string, unterminated comment, unterminated regexp, raw newline in a string,\n  bad escape sequences (`\\x` / `\\u` with non-hex or too few digits, `\\` at EOF),\n  number with leading zero (legacy octal), number with letter suffix, missing\n  exponent digits, illegal/unexpected characters, bad UTF-8 in the source,\n  unterminated character class in a regexp literal, `expected '<c>'` cases.\n\n`errors_parser_compiler.txt` — ERRORS.md sections 13-14 (`jsparse.c`, `jscompile.c`,\n  rows ~277-319): unexpected token cases, `unmatched '('`/`')'`, missing `;`,\n  invalid assignment target / `invalid left-hand side`, `break`/`continue` outside a\n  loop, `continue` in a `switch`, duplicate `default` in a switch, label errors\n  (unknown label, duplicate label), `return` outside a function, the always-reserved\n  future words (`class const enum export extends import super`), the strict-only\n  future reserved words (`implements interface let package private protected public\n  static yield`), duplicate formal parameters in strict mode, duplicate object-literal\n  properties in strict mode, `delete` on an unqualified name in strict mode,\n  `with` in strict mode, assigning to `arguments`/`eval`, declaring `arguments`/`eval`,\n  the AST/nesting depth limit (JS_ASTLIMIT), and `too many` / `program too large`\n  compiler limits.\n\n`errors_runtime.txt` — ERRORS.md sections 7-11 (`jsrun.c`, `jsproperty.c`, rows\n  ~132-250): calling a non-function, `new` on a non-constructor, property access on\n  `null`/`undefined`, `cannot convert undefined to object`, ReferenceError for an\n  undefined variable read, read-only / non-configurable / non-extensible violations\n  in strict mode, `in`/`instanceof` on non-objects, `instanceof` with a\n  non-callable RHS, invalid array length (`new Array(-1)`, `a.length=-1`,\n  `a.length=1.5`), the JS_ARRAYLIMIT boundary, `too much recursion` /\n  environment-limit overflow via deep recursion, `script ran too long`\n  (not reachable without js_setlimit -- skip if so), cyclic prototype chains,\n  getter/setter misuse, and `js_insert`-style \"not implemented\" paths reachable\n  from JS if any.\n\n`errors_builtins.txt` — ERRORS.md sections 15-16 (rows ~320-445): `new RegExp` with\n  an invalid pattern (one snippet per distinct `regexp.c` `die()` message reachable\n  through `new RegExp(...)`), invalid regexp flags, `Number.prototype.toString`\n  with an out-of-range radix, `toFixed`/`toExponential`/`toPrecision` with\n  out-of-range digit counts, `Number.prototype` methods invoked on a non-Number\n  `this`, likewise for `String.prototype`/`Boolean.prototype`/`Date.prototype`/\n  `Array.prototype`/`RegExp.prototype` methods called on the wrong `this` type,\n  `Object.defineProperty` with a bad descriptor (both `value` and `get`),\n  `Object.defineProperty` on a non-object, `Object.create` with a bad prototype,\n  `JSON.parse` syntax errors (one per distinct `json.c` message),\n  `JSON.stringify` of a cyclic object, `decodeURI`/`decodeURIComponent` with a\n  truncated or invalid `%` escape, `Function` constructor with an invalid body,\n  `Array.prototype.reduce` on an empty array with no initial value,\n  `Function.prototype.apply` with a non-array-like, and `String.prototype.replace`\n  with a bad `$` pattern if the C rejects it.\n\n`builtins_regexp.txt` — VALID-PATH RegExp snippets (this file must NOT error).\n  Cover: `new RegExp(pattern)` and `new RegExp(pattern, flags)` and the literal form;\n  all 8 combinations of the `g`/`i`/`m` flags; `RegExp.prototype.exec` returning\n  `null` vs a match array (checking `index`, `input`, `length` and each capture,\n  including unmatched optional captures which must be `undefined`);\n  `RegExp.prototype.test`; the `lastIndex` bookkeeping across repeated `exec` calls\n  with and without `g` (including manually setting `lastIndex` to a value past the\n  end, to a negative, and to a fractional number); the `source`/`global`/\n  `ignoreCase`/`multiline`/`lastIndex` properties; `RegExp.prototype.toString`;\n  `String.prototype.match/replace/search/split` with regexps; and one snippet per\n  pattern construct the engine supports (read `c_src/src/regexp.c`): `.`, classes,\n  negated classes, ranges, `^ $ \\b \\B`, `\\d \\D \\w \\W \\s \\S`, `\\xHH`, `\\uHHHH`,\n  `\\cX`, backreferences, alternation, capturing and non-capturing groups,\n  positive and negative lookahead, all greedy and lazy quantifiers, and nesting.\n\n`builtins_json.txt` — VALID-PATH JSON snippets. Cover `JSON.parse` of every value\n  type (numbers in all forms, strings with every escape incl. `\\uHHHH` and\n  surrogate pairs, `true/false/null`, nested arrays and objects, empty array and\n  object, deeply nested, whitespace variations) and with a `reviver` function;\n  `JSON.stringify` of every value type plus `undefined`, functions, `NaN`,\n  `Infinity`, `-0`, `Date` objects, `Number`/`String`/`Boolean` wrappers, objects\n  with a `toJSON` method, nested structures, sparse arrays, non-enumerable\n  properties; and the `replacer` argument as both a function and an array, and the\n  `space` argument as 0, a number 1..10, a number out of range (negative and > 10),\n  a short string, and a string longer than 10 characters.\n\n`builtins_date.txt` — VALID-PATH Date snippets. IMPORTANT: avoid anything depending\n  on the CURRENT time or the local timezone where the result could differ between\n  two runs -- prefer explicit UTC timestamps and the `getUTC*` accessors, and for\n  local-time functions only use snippets whose value is timezone-independent\n  (or note that both builds run in the same process so the TZ is identical -- local\n  time IS comparable, but `Date.now()`/`new Date()` with no args is NOT).\n  Cover: `new Date(ms)` for 0, negative, huge, NaN, and the +/-8.64e15 valid-range\n  boundary; `new Date(y,m,d,h,mi,s,ms)` with in-range and out-of-range (overflowing)\n  components and with 2..7 arguments; `new Date(string)` for the ISO forms the C\n  parses; `Date.parse`, `Date.UTC`, and every `Date.prototype` getter and setter in\n  `c_src/src/jsdate.c` (`getTime getFullYear getMonth getDate getDay getHours\n  getMinutes getSeconds getMilliseconds getTimezoneOffset` and their `getUTC*`\n  variants, plus `setTime setFullYear setMonth setDate setHours setMinutes\n  setSeconds setMilliseconds` and the `setUTC*` variants with 1..N arguments),\n  and `toString toDateString toTimeString toUTCString toISOString toJSON\n  valueOf toLocaleString toLocaleDateString toLocaleTimeString`.\n\nHOW TO VERIFY (do this before finishing — it is required):\nThe C shared library is already built at `c_src/build/libmujs.so`. Write a small\nC driver to check your corpora. For example:\n\n```bash\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jqRrno\ncat > /tmp/chk.c <<'EOF'\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include \"mujs.h\"\nstatic void rep(js_State *J, const char *m){ printf(\"  ERR %s\\n\", m); }\nint main(int argc, char **argv){\n  char line[65536]; FILE *f = fopen(argv[1], \"r\"); int n=0, bad=0;\n  while (fgets(line, sizeof line, f)) {\n    int L = strlen(line); while (L && (line[L-1]=='\\n'||line[L-1]=='\\r')) line[--L]=0;\n    if (!L || line[0]=='#') continue;\n    /* expand \\n and \\t like the Rust harness does */\n    char src[65536]; int o=0;\n    for (int i=0;i<L;i++){ if(line[i]=='\\\\'&&line[i+1]=='n'){src[o++]='\\n';i++;}\n      else if(line[i]=='\\\\'&&line[i+1]=='t'){src[o++]='\\t';i++;} else src[o++]=line[i]; }\n    src[o]=0;\n    n++;\n    int errored = 0;\n    for (int strict=0; strict<2; strict++) {\n      js_State *J = js_newstate(NULL, NULL, strict);\n      js_setreport(J, NULL);\n      if (js_ploadstring(J, \"[string]\", src)) { errored=1; js_pop(J,1); }\n      else { js_pushundefined(J); if (js_pcall(J,0)) errored=1; js_pop(J,1); }\n      js_freestate(J);\n    }\n    if (argc>2 && !errored) { printf(\"NOT-ERRORING: %s\\n\", line); bad++; }\n    if (argc<=2 && errored) { printf(\"ERRORS(unexpected for valid corpus): %s\\n\", line); bad++; }\n  }\n  printf(\"checked %d snippets, %d flagged\\n\", n, bad);\n  return 0;\n}\nEOF\ngcc -I c_src/include -o /tmp/chk /tmp/chk.c c_src/build/libmujs.so -lm -Wl,-rpath,$PWD/c_src/build\n# error corpora: pass a 2nd arg -> flags snippets that DO NOT error\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/errors_lexer.txt x\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/errors_parser_compiler.txt x\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/errors_runtime.txt x\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/errors_builtins.txt x\n# valid corpora: no 2nd arg -> flags snippets that DO error\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/builtins_regexp.txt\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/builtins_json.txt\n/tmp/chk translation/tests/corpus/builtins_date.txt\nEOF\n```\nIterate until the four `errors_*` files report `0 flagged`. For the three valid\ncorpora, a small number of intentionally-throwing snippets is acceptable, but\nreview each flagged line and remove it if it was unintentional.\n\nNote: `$TMPDIR` is the writable temp dir in this sandbox -- use `$TMPDIR` instead of\n`/tmp` if `/tmp` is not writable.\n\nReport at the end, per file: total lines, snippet count, and the verifier's\n\"checked N snippets, M flagged\" output."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10108,"cache_read_input_tokens":264354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10108,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"365846c7-7961-47ff-8218-f2422469d650","uuid":"fc89d97d-d264-450b-b62b-e15eaaee8330","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:05:11.329Z"}
