{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"btwdt41bw","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0194kdKDDx2uHjiUa8sVnB5Y","description":"Run API error tests","is_backgrounded":false,"task_type":"local_bash","uuid":"7ba9b206-2f71-4ffd-ad74-97cd534297be","session_id":"365846c7-7961-47ff-8218-f2422469d650"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nzpxk2yjee23sq4klunzvzo6hpphb54eogncqnvz7ama6mssmlia","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's the audit.\n\n## Method (exhaustive, not sampled)\n\nRather than grepping ~974 integer-target `as` casts by hand, I used the compiler to enumerate float-source casts exactly: `cargo clippy --release --lib -- -A clippy::all -W clippy::cast_possible_truncation -W clippy::cast_sign_loss`, filtering for `casting \\`f64\\`/\\`f32\\` to ...`. I first validated that this lint fires **unconditionally** on every float→int cast — including in-range constants, already-range-guarded expressions, all widths (i8/i16/i32/i64/isize/u8/u16/u32/u64/usize), and casts inside local `macro_rules!` expansions — so the list is complete. All 32 modules are in `lib.rs`, so nothing was excluded from the analysis. That yielded **exactly 19 warnings covering 18 distinct cast sites**, each of which I matched to its C line.\n\n## Table of every float-source cast examined\n\n| Rust site | C site | C source → dest types | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `jsarray.rs:329` (`s = ... as c_int`) | `jsarray.c:269` `s = sv<0?0:sv>len?len:sv` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — `sv` comes from `js_tointeger` which returns **`int`** (jsrun.c:328), so never NaN and always exact; ternary clamps to `[0,len]` |\n| `jsarray.rs:336` (`e = ... as c_int`) | `jsarray.c:270` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — same reasoning |\n| `jscompile.rs:245` (`num as c_int`) | `jscompile.c:163` `num == (int)num` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — `&&` short-circuits behind `num >= SHRT_MIN && num <= SHRT_MAX` |\n| `jscompile.rs:247` (`(num+32768.0) as c_int`) | `jscompile.c:165` `emitarg(J,F,num+32768)` | `double`→`int` param | **OK** — same guard ⇒ value in `[0,65535]` |\n| `jscompile.rs:764` (`(*exp).number as c_int`) | `jscompile.c:609` `emitarg(J,F,exp->number)` | `double`→`int` param | **OK** — `EXP_REGEXP.number` is set only from `J->number` = regexp flag bits (jslex.c:529-533), range 0..7 |\n| `jsdate.rs:21` (`x as c_int` inside `d2i`) | (helper) | — | **OK** — this *is* the faithful `cvttsd2si` model; guarded `[-2^31, 2^31)` else `INT_MIN` |\n| `jsdtoa.rs:259` (`ceil(...) as c_int`) | `jsdtoa.c:380` `return ceil((alpha-e+63)*D_1_LOG2_10)` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — `alpha=-59`, `e=w_p.e+64`, `\\|w_p.e\\|≲1140` ⇒ result ≲ ±400 |\n| `jsgc.rs:331` (`(remaining as f64 * JS_GCFACTOR) as c_uint`) | `jsgc.c:250` `J->gcthresh = remaining * JS_GCFACTOR` | `double`→`unsigned int` (jsi.h:266), **unguarded** | **BUG** — fixed |\n| `jsnumber.rs:105` (`... as u64`) | `jsnumber.c:65` `u = number*pow(radix,exp)+0.5` | `double`→`uint64_t` | **OK** — the two preceding `while` loops bound the value by `limit = 1<<52`; `number>0`, NaN/inf/0 returned earlier. Also verified the denormal/`pow`-overflows-to-inf path gives `u=0` in both |\n| `jsparse.rs:1285` (`(d-two32) as c_int`) | `jsparse.c:968` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — `d ∈ [2^31, 2^32)` ⇒ `d-two32 ∈ [-2^31, 0)` |\n| `jsparse.rs:1287` (`d as c_int`) | `jsparse.c:970` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — `d ∈ [0, 2^31)`; non-finite/0 returned earlier |\n| `jsrun.rs:917` (`... as c_int as c_ushort`) | `jsrun.c:750` | `double`→`unsigned short` | **OK** — the already-applied fix; `jsV_tointeger` pre-clamps to `[INT_MIN,INT_MAX]` and is never NaN, so the intermediate `as c_int` is exact |\n| `jsrun.rs:1991` (`(*v).u.number as c_int`) | `jsrun.c:1569` `*k = v->u.number` | `double`→`int`, **unguarded** | **BUG** — fixed |\n| `jsvalue.rs:75` (`n as c_int`) | `jsvalue.c:48` `return (int)n` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — explicit `isnan`/`< INT_MIN`/`> INT_MAX` guards precede it |\n| `jsvalue.rs:91` (`(n-two32) as c_int`) | `jsvalue.c:68` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — as jsparse |\n| `jsvalue.rs:93` (`n as c_int`) | `jsvalue.c:70` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — as jsparse |\n| `jsvalue.rs:374` (`f as c_int`) | `jsvalue.c:283` `int i = (int)f` | `double`→`int` | **OK** — guarded by `f >= INT_MIN && f <= INT_MAX` |\n\nAlso cross-checked from the C side so nothing could hide behind a *missing* Rust cast: every explicit narrowing cast in `c_src/src/*.c`, and every use of the double-returning APIs (`jsV_tonumber`, `jsV_tointeger`, `js_tonumber`, `floor/ceil/fmod/pow/fabs`, `TimeClip`, `MakeDay/MakeTime/MakeDate`, `pmod`, `LocalTZA`, …). Key finding that eliminates most suspects: **`js_tointeger`/`js_toint32`/`js_touint32`/`js_tryinteger` return integer types in C**, not `double`, so `int pos = js_tointeger(...)` sites in `jsstring.c`/`jsarray.c`/`jsnumber.c` involve no float conversion at all. `jsV_numbertoint16`/`uint16` are `int`→`short`/`unsigned short`, which Rust `as` truncates identically.\n\n`jsdate.rs` specifically: I verified all 8 C functions that implicitly convert `double`→`int` (`Day`, `DayFromYear`, `YearFromTime`, `WeekDay`, `HourFromTime`, `MinFromTime`, `SecFromTime`, `msFromTime`) plus the three conversions in `MakeDay` (`im = (int)m`, `TimeFromYear(y)`, `DaysInYear(y)`) and `tza` in `parseDateTime` all route through `d2i`. The `MakeDay` NaN path matters and is correct: `d2i(NaN) = INT_MIN` trips the `im < 0` guard and returns `NAN`, whereas a bare `NaN as i32` would have given 0 and silently produced a valid date.\n\n## Bug class (B): no findings\n\nGrepped all of `src/` for `try_into`, `.clamp(`, `saturating_*`, `checked_*`, `.min(`, `.max(`, `unwrap()`. All `wrapping_*` uses are correct C-matching wraparound; the only `unwrap()`s are on `Option<js_Alloc>` function pointers, and the C guards `if (!alloc) alloc = js_defaultalloc;` (jsstate.c:194) before any call, so there is no Rust-only panic path. No Rust-introduced clamp, saturate, or range guard that the C lacks.\n\n## Fixes applied (2)\n\n**`translation/src/jsgc.rs:331`** — `remaining * JS_GCFACTOR` (double) stored into `unsigned int gcthresh`. C wraps mod 2^32 (`cvttsd2si` to a 64-bit reg, low 32 bits kept); Rust `f64 as u32` saturates at 4294967295. Diverges once `remaining > 858993459`.\n```rust\n(*J).gcthresh = (remaining as f64 * JS_GCFACTOR) as i64 as c_uint;\n```\n\n**`translation/src/jsrun.rs:1991`** — `jsR_isindex`'s `*k = v->u.number;` is a fully unguarded `double`→`int`. Replaced the saturating cast with the explicit `cvttsd2si` model (`INT_MIN` for NaN and for anything outside `[-2^31, 2^31)`), matching `jsdate.rs`'s `d2i`. Note: I confirmed the function's *return value* was already identical in both (the converted int can only compare equal to the original double when that double is an exactly-representable in-range integer, where both conversions agree), so this was a latent rather than currently-observable divergence — but the value written through `*k` did differ, and the fix costs nothing.\n\n## Verification\n\n`cargo build --release` — `Finished \\`release\\` profile [optimized] target(s)`.\n\n`cargo test --release` cannot link as a whole: `tests/b06_scripts.rs` fails to **compile** because `include_str!` references six `tests/corpus/*.txt` files that don't exist yet (the other agent's work). All other targets run clean:\n\n```\na00_harness            test result: ok. 4 passed;  0 failed\nb01_utf                test result: ok. 16 passed; 0 failed\nb02_number             test result: ok. 17 passed; 0 failed\nb03_regexp             test result: ok. 20 passed; 0 failed\nb04_lexer_strhelpers   test result: ok. 8 passed;  0 failed\nb05_stackapi           test result: ok. 28 passed; 0 failed\nb07_random             test result: ok. 8 passed;  0 failed\nb08_numeric_torture    test result: ok. 12 passed; 0 failed\n```\n(113 differential assertions passing, 0 failures. `b08_numeric_torture` had appeared since the task was written and compiles fine, so I ran it too — it includes a `regexp_lastindex_numeric_torture` case, which covers the original `jsrun.c:911` bug.) Nothing under `translation/tests/` was modified and no test files were created."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":936,"cache_read_input_tokens":76318,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":936,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UPcLwF7Z7A5hxvPHhzEkmA","session_id":"365846c7-7961-47ff-8218-f2422469d650","uuid":"cc09d153-73a3-4093-8309-b3a0700bfbe6","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:15:36.318Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit float-to-int casts vs C"}
