{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_lbcf4i57z42373mf45ltxoc5itn2mk7qraaxmdbfmzgywdpoomja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_017paV71bN589NrGdScb7upb","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit argument evaluation order","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Working dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jqRrno\n\nContext: `translation/src/*.rs` is a Rust translation of the C in `c_src/src/*.c` (MuJS).\nIt must be BYTE-IDENTICAL to the C for all inputs. The C is ALWAYS correct.\nNEVER modify anything under `c_src/`. Do NOT touch anything under `translation/tests/`\n(another agent owns the tests). Only edit `translation/src/*.rs`.\n\nA REAL, OBSERVABLE bug of this exact class was just found and fixed. Study it:\n\nC (`c_src/src/jsrun.c:1011`):\n```c\nvoid js_setproperty(js_State *J, int idx, const char *name)\n{\n    jsR_setproperty(J, js_toobject(J, idx), name, !js_isobject(J, idx));\n    js_pop(J, 1);\n}\n```\n`js_toobject(J, idx)` has a SIDE EFFECT: it REPLACES the value in stack slot `idx`\nwith its wrapper object. C leaves the order of evaluation of function arguments\nUNSPECIFIED, and gcc on x86-64 evaluates them RIGHT TO LEFT, so `!js_isobject(J, idx)`\nruns FIRST and sees the ORIGINAL primitive (giving transient = 1). The Rust translation\nhad written the arguments in source order, and Rust GUARANTEES LEFT-TO-RIGHT evaluation,\nso `js_toobject` ran first, boxed the slot, and `js_isobject` then returned 1, giving\ntransient = 0 -- which silently lost the strict-mode\n`TypeError \"cannot create property '%s' on transient object\"`.\n\nThe applied fix sequences the calls explicitly to match gcc's order:\n```rust\nlet transient = (js_isobject(J, idx) == 0) as c_int;\nlet obj = js_toobject(J, idx);\njsR_setproperty(J, obj, name, transient);\n```\nThe same fix was applied at `js_setindex` (C: `jsrun.c:1049`).\n\nYOUR TASK: find EVERY OTHER place in the C where a single expression contains TWO OR MORE\nsub-expressions with side effects whose relative order is unspecified in C, and verify\nthat the Rust translation reproduces gcc's actual order. Fix any mismatch.\n\nWhat counts as a side effect here:\n- any call that pushes to / pops from / mutates the mujs value stack\n  (`js_push*`, `js_pop`, `js_to*` -- note `js_toobject`, `js_tostring`, `js_tonumber`\n  and friends can BOX or otherwise REWRITE the slot -- `js_call`, `js_construct`,\n  `js_next*`, `js_concat`, `js_dup*`, `js_rot*`, `js_copy`, `js_remove`, `js_replace`)\n- any call that allocates or can throw (`js_malloc`, `js_realloc`, `js_intern`,\n  `jsV_new*`, anything that can `longjmp`)\n- `++`/`--`, compound assignment, or assignment to the same object twice in one\n  expression\n- reads of a variable that another sub-expression of the same expression writes\n\nWhere to look (be systematic — go file by file through `c_src/src/*.c`):\n1. Function CALL argument lists containing two or more of the above calls. Grep for\n   lines with two `js_`/`jsV_`/`jsR_`/`jsP_`/`jsC_`/`jsY_` calls on the same line,\n   then read each to judge whether the order matters.\n2. Binary operators with side effects on both sides: `a() + b()`, `a() - b()`,\n   `a() == b()`, `a() < b()`, `a() , b()` — but note `&&`, `||` and `?:` DO have\n   defined short-circuit order in C, so those are fine as long as the Rust also\n   short-circuits (verify the Rust uses `&&`/`||`/`if` and not eager evaluation into\n   temporaries!).\n3. Assignments whose LHS subscript/deref and RHS both have side effects, e.g.\n   `STACK[TOP++] = f(J)` or `*p++ = g()`.\n4. Initialiser lists and `return f(g(), h());`.\n5. `printf`/`sprintf`/`js_error`-style variadic calls with multiple side-effecting args.\n\nImportant nuance to check in BOTH directions:\n- The Rust may have introduced a temporary that changes the order (the bug above).\n- The Rust may ALSO have reordered a short-circuit into eager evaluation, which would\n  make it evaluate something the C never evaluates (e.g. C `if (a && b(J))` translated\n  as `let x = b(J); if a != 0 && x != 0`). That is equally a bug — the extra call can\n  push/allocate/throw.\n\nTo determine gcc's ACTUAL order when it matters, you can compile a tiny probe:\n```bash\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-jqRrno\ncat > $TMPDIR/order.c <<'EOF'\n#include <stdio.h>\nstatic int n = 0;\nstatic int a(void){ printf(\"a=%d\\n\", ++n); return n; }\nstatic int b(void){ printf(\"b=%d\\n\", ++n); return n; }\nstatic void f(int x, int y){ printf(\"f(%d,%d)\\n\", x, y); }\nint main(void){ f(a(), b()); return 0; }\nEOF\ngcc -O2 -o $TMPDIR/order $TMPDIR/order.c && $TMPDIR/order\n```\n(Use `$TMPDIR`, not `/tmp`.) Also verify the real library's behaviour empirically where\nyou can, by building a small C driver against the already-built\n`c_src/build/libmujs.so` (include dir `c_src/include`, link `-lm`).\n\nFor each fix, add a comment in the style of the applied fix above, naming the C file:line\nand explaining why the order matters.\n\nVERIFY YOUR WORK:\n- `cd translation && cargo build --release` must succeed.\n- Run the differential suite (it loads both .so files and compares them):\n  `cd translation && for t in a00_harness b01_utf b02_number b03_regexp b04_lexer_strhelpers b05_stackapi b07_random b08_numeric_torture c09_api_errors; do echo \"== $t\"; timeout 600 cargo test --release --test $t 2>&1 | grep -E 'test result|FAILED|diverged'; done`\n  All targets must report `ok`. (Skip the `b06_scripts` target: it depends on corpus\n  files another agent is still writing.)\n\nREPORT: a table of every multi-side-effect expression you examined (C file:line, the\nexpression, whether order matters, gcc's order, the Rust order, verdict OK/BUG, fix\napplied). Then the test summary. If you find no additional bugs, say so explicitly and\nshow what you checked."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_cr